WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.250
- Welcome to the Huberman Lab Podcast,

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:03.690
where we discuss science

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:05.913
and science-based tools for everyday life.

00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:10.380
I'm Andrew Huberman,

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:13.230
and I'm a professor of
neurobiology and ophthalmology

00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:15.000
at Stanford School of Medicine.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:17.430
Today, my guest is Dr. David Anderson,

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:19.380
Dr. Anderson is a professor of biology

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:21.960
at the California Institute of Technology,

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:25.440
often commonly referred
to as Caltech University.

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:28.050
Dr. Anderson's research
focuses on emotions

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:30.000
and states of mind and body,

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.830
and indeed he emphasizes how emotions,

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:34.560
like happiness, sadness, anger and so on,

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:36.270
are actually subcategories

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:39.330
of what are generally governed by states,

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:41.250
that is, things that are occurring

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:43.020
in the nervous system in our brain

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:45.750
and in the connections
between brain and body

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:46.710
that dictate whether or not

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:48.780
we feel good about how we are feeling,

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:50.640
and that drive our behaviors,

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:54.540
that is, bias us to be
in action or inaction

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:56.820
and strongly influence
the way we interpret

00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:58.680
our experience and our surroundings.

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:01.140
Today, Dr. Anderson
teaches us, for instance,

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:02.670
why people become aggressive

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:06.900
and why that aggression can
sometimes take the form of rage.

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:08.880
We also talk about sexual behavior,

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:11.250
and the boundaries and overlap

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:13.620
between aggression and sexual behavior.

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:16.590
And that discussion about
aggression and sexual behavior

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:19.110
also starts to focus on particular aspects

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:21.360
of neural circuits and
states of mind and body

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:23.670
that govern things like, for instance,

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:24.780
male-male aggression,

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:26.400
versus male-female aggression,

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:28.770
versus female-female aggression.

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:30.060
So today, you will learn a lot

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:31.470
about the biological mechanisms

00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:33.900
that govern why we feel the way we feel.

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:35.820
Indeed, Dr. Anderson is an author

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:37.860
of a terrific new popular book,

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:41.010
entitled "The Nature of the
Beast: How Emotions Guide Us".

00:01:41.010 --> 00:01:43.093
I've read this book several times now,

00:01:43.093 --> 00:01:45.030
I can tell you it contains so many gems

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:47.520
that are firmly grounded
in the scientific research.

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:49.500
In fact, a lot of what's in the book

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:51.690
contrasts with many of the common myths

00:01:51.690 --> 00:01:53.910
about emotions and biology.

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:55.410
So whether or not you're a therapist,

00:01:55.410 --> 00:01:56.520
or you're a biologist,

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:58.500
or you're simply just somebody interested

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:00.210
in why we feel the way we feel

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:02.100
and why we act the way we act,

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:03.900
I cannot recommend the book highly enough.

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:04.733
Again, the title is

00:02:04.733 --> 00:02:07.200
"The Nature of the Beast:
How Emotions Guide Us".

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:09.480
Today's discussion also
ventures into topics

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:12.120
such as mental health and mental illness,

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:13.950
and some of the exciting discoveries

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:15.720
that have been made by
Dr. Anderson's laboratory

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:18.600
and other laboratories
identifying specific peptides,

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:20.430
that is, small proteins

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:21.960
that can govern whether or not people

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:23.670
feel anxious or less anxious,

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:25.350
aggressive or less aggressive.

00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:27.120
This is an important area of research

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:28.470
that has direct implications

00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:30.570
for much of what we
read about in the news,

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:33.060
both unfortunate and fortunate events,

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:35.580
and that will no doubt drive the future

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:37.050
of mental health treatments.

00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:40.620
Dr. Anderson is considered
one of the most pioneering

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:43.830
and important researchers
in neurobiology of our time.

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:45.960
Indeed, he is a member of the
National Academy of Sciences

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:49.110
and a Howard Hughes Medical
Institute investigator.

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:51.420
I've mentioned the HHMI
once or twice before

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:54.450
when we've had other HHMI
guests on this podcast,

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:55.860
but for those of you
that are not familiar,

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:57.210
the Howard Hughes Medical Institute

00:02:57.210 --> 00:02:59.370
funds a small number of investigators

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:02.790
doing particularly
high-risk, high-benefit work,

00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:05.580
and it is an extremely competitive process

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:08.790
to identify those Howard
Hughes investigators.

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:09.720
They are essentially appointed,

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:11.550
and then every five years,

00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:13.740
they have to compete against one another

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:16.080
and against a new incoming flock

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:18.690
of would-be HHMI investigators

00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:19.920
to get another five years of funding.

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:22.110
They are literally given
a grade every five years

00:03:22.110 --> 00:03:24.570
as to whether or not they
can continue, not continue,

00:03:24.570 --> 00:03:26.010
or whether or not they should worry

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:28.530
about being funded for an
extended period of time.

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:30.300
Dr. Anderson has been an investigator

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:33.870
with the Howard Hughes
Medical Institute since 1989.

