00:08:07.380 --> 00:08:10.770
And now for my discussion
with Dr. David Anderson.

00:08:10.770 --> 00:08:11.850
David, great to be here

00:08:11.850 --> 00:08:14.130
and great to finally sit
down and chat with you.

00:08:14.130 --> 00:08:16.020
- Great to be here too, thank you so much.

00:08:16.020 --> 00:08:17.430
- Yeah, I have a ton of questions,

00:08:17.430 --> 00:08:20.280
but I want to start with
something fairly basic,

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:23.640
but that I'm aware is a
pretty vast landscape,

00:08:23.640 --> 00:08:27.660
and that's the difference
between emotions and states,

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:28.950
if indeed there is a difference,

00:08:28.950 --> 00:08:31.890
and how we should think about emotions.

00:08:31.890 --> 00:08:33.540
What are they?

00:08:33.540 --> 00:08:34.500
They have all these names,

00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:38.760
happiness, sadness,
depression, anger, rage,

00:08:38.760 --> 00:08:39.960
how should we think about them

00:08:39.960 --> 00:08:42.750
and why might states be

00:08:42.750 --> 00:08:45.450
at least as useful a thing to think about,

00:08:45.450 --> 00:08:46.950
if not more useful?

00:08:46.950 --> 00:08:48.390
- That's great.

00:08:48.390 --> 00:08:50.400
First, the short answer to your question

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:55.400
is that I see emotions as
a type of internal state,

00:08:55.400 --> 00:08:58.870
in the sense that arousal's
also a type of internal state,

00:08:58.870 --> 00:09:01.290
motivation's a type of internal state,

00:09:01.290 --> 00:09:04.230
sleep is a type of internal state.

00:09:04.230 --> 00:09:06.720
And the sort of simplest way

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:09.210
I think of internal states is that,

00:09:09.210 --> 00:09:10.500
as you've shown in your own work,

00:09:10.500 --> 00:09:15.360
they change the input-to-output
transformation of the brain.

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:18.240
When you're asleep, you
don't hear something

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:20.130
that you would hear if you were awake,

00:09:20.130 --> 00:09:22.260
unless it's a really, really loud noise.

00:09:22.260 --> 00:09:25.050
So from that broad perspective,

00:09:25.050 --> 00:09:28.350
I see emotion as a class of state

00:09:28.350 --> 00:09:30.210
that controls behavior.

00:09:30.210 --> 00:09:33.810
The reason I think it's useful
to think about it as a state

00:09:33.810 --> 00:09:38.810
is it puts the focus on it
as a neurobiological process

00:09:39.300 --> 00:09:42.600
rather than as a psychological process.

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:46.470
And this gets around all of
the definitional problems

00:09:46.470 --> 00:09:49.560
that people have with the word emotion,

00:09:49.560 --> 00:09:53.430
where many people equate
emotion with feeling,

00:09:53.430 --> 00:09:55.620
which is a subjective sense

00:09:55.620 --> 00:09:59.040
that we can only study in humans,

00:09:59.040 --> 00:10:01.740
because to find out
what someone's feeling,

00:10:01.740 --> 00:10:02.790
you have to ask them,

00:10:02.790 --> 00:10:05.610
and people are the only
animals that can talk,

00:10:05.610 --> 00:10:07.230
that we can understand.

00:10:07.230 --> 00:10:10.743
So that's how I think about emotion,

00:10:12.190 --> 00:10:13.380
if you think of an iceberg,

00:10:13.380 --> 00:10:15.540
it's the part of the iceberg

00:10:15.540 --> 00:10:18.060
that's below the surface of the water,

00:10:18.060 --> 00:10:20.220
the feeling part is the tip

00:10:20.220 --> 00:10:21.630
that's sort of floating above

00:10:21.630 --> 00:10:23.700
the surface of your consciousness.

00:10:23.700 --> 00:10:25.770
Not that that isn't important, it is,

00:10:25.770 --> 00:10:28.260
but you have to understand consciousness

00:10:28.260 --> 00:10:30.360
if you want to understand feelings,

00:10:30.360 --> 00:10:33.810
and we're not ready to
study that in animals yet,

00:10:33.810 --> 00:10:36.420
and so that's how I think about it.

00:10:36.420 --> 00:10:39.063
- What are the different
components of a state?

00:10:40.140 --> 00:10:43.590
You mentioned arousal as a key component,

00:10:43.590 --> 00:10:45.810
what are some of the
other features of states

00:10:45.810 --> 00:10:50.250
that represent this, as you so
beautifully put in your book,

00:10:50.250 --> 00:10:51.780
that represent below the tip

00:10:51.780 --> 00:10:53.220
of the iceberg?
- Right, right.

00:10:53.220 --> 00:10:58.220
So you can break states
up into different facets,

00:11:01.830 --> 00:11:03.840
or people would call them dimensions,

00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:06.030
and so there have been people

00:11:06.030 --> 00:11:08.250
who have thought of emotions

00:11:08.250 --> 00:11:11.330
as having just really two dimensions,

00:11:11.330 --> 00:11:16.330
an arousal dimension, how intense is it?

00:11:16.680 --> 00:11:20.280
And also a valence dimension,

00:11:20.280 --> 00:11:24.120
which is, is it positive
or negative, good or bad?

00:11:24.120 --> 00:11:25.920
Ralph Adolphs and I have tried

00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:28.350
to expand that a little bit

00:11:28.350 --> 00:11:31.050
to think about components of emotion,

00:11:31.050 --> 00:11:34.110
particularly those that
distinguish emotion states

00:11:34.110 --> 00:11:36.120
from motivational states,

00:11:36.120 --> 00:11:38.583
because they are very closely related.

00:11:39.630 --> 00:11:44.040
One of those important
properties is persistence,

00:11:44.040 --> 00:11:46.530
and this is something that distinguishes

00:11:46.530 --> 00:11:50.340
state-driven behaviors
from simple reflexes.

00:11:50.340 --> 00:11:55.340
Reflexes tend to terminate
when the stimulus turns off,

00:11:55.770 --> 00:11:58.470
like the doctor hitting
your knee with a hammer,

00:11:58.470 --> 00:12:00.900
it initiates with the stimulus onset

00:12:00.900 --> 00:12:03.660
and it terminates with
the stimulus offset,

00:12:03.660 --> 00:12:06.180
emotions tend to outlast, often,

00:12:06.180 --> 00:12:08.340
the stimulus that evoke them.

00:12:08.340 --> 00:12:12.510
If you're walking along a trail
here in Southern California,

00:12:12.510 --> 00:12:15.150
you hear a rattlesnake rattling,

00:12:15.150 --> 00:12:16.920
you're going to jump in the air,

00:12:16.920 --> 00:12:19.260
but your heart is going
to continue to beat

00:12:19.260 --> 00:12:22.530
and your palms sweat and
your mouth is going to be dry

00:12:22.530 --> 00:12:25.440
for a while after it's
slithered off in the bush,

00:12:25.440 --> 00:12:27.210
and you're going to be hypervigilant,

00:12:27.210 --> 00:12:28.410
if you see something

00:12:28.410 --> 00:12:31.620
that even remotely looks
snake-like, a stick,

00:12:31.620 --> 00:12:33.510
you're going to stop and jump.

00:12:33.510 --> 00:12:38.453
So persistence is an important
feature of emotion states,

00:12:39.510 --> 00:12:42.450
not all states have persistence.

00:12:42.450 --> 00:12:45.390
So for example, you think about hunger.

00:12:45.390 --> 00:12:48.450
Once you've eaten, the state is gone,

00:12:48.450 --> 00:12:50.070
you're not hungry anymore,

00:12:50.070 --> 00:12:52.350
but if you are really angry

00:12:52.350 --> 00:12:54.330
and you get into a fight with somebody,

00:12:54.330 --> 00:12:56.070
even after the fight is over,

00:12:56.070 --> 00:12:59.787
you may remain riled up for a long time

00:12:59.787 --> 00:13:02.280
and it takes you a while to calm down,

00:13:02.280 --> 00:13:05.310
and that may have to do
with the arousal dimension

00:13:05.310 --> 00:13:06.633
or some other part of it.

00:13:07.500 --> 00:13:10.320
And then generalization

00:13:10.320 --> 00:13:15.320
is an important component of
emotion states that make them,

00:13:17.040 --> 00:13:21.330
if they have been
triggered in one situation,

00:13:21.330 --> 00:13:24.360
they can apply to another situation.

00:13:24.360 --> 00:13:26.490
And my favorite example of that is,

00:13:26.490 --> 00:13:29.970
you come home from work
and your kid is screaming,

00:13:29.970 --> 00:13:31.560
if you had a good day at work,

00:13:31.560 --> 00:13:33.687
you might pick it up and sooth it,

00:13:33.687 --> 00:13:35.910
and if you had a bad day at work,

00:13:35.910 --> 00:13:39.270
you might react very differently
to it and scream at it.

00:13:39.270 --> 00:13:41.430
And so that's a generalization

00:13:41.430 --> 00:13:44.130
of the state that was triggered at work,

00:13:44.130 --> 00:13:46.050
by something your boss said to you,

00:13:46.050 --> 00:13:48.870
to a completely different interaction.

00:13:48.870 --> 00:13:51.450
And again, that's something
that distinguishes

00:13:51.450 --> 00:13:54.420
emotion states from motivation states,

00:13:54.420 --> 00:13:57.210
motivation states are really specific,

00:13:57.210 --> 00:13:59.310
find and eat food,

00:13:59.310 --> 00:14:02.010
obtain and consume water,

00:14:02.010 --> 00:14:06.930
and they're involved in
homeostatic maintenance.

00:14:06.930 --> 00:14:11.880
So states are very multifaceted,

00:14:11.880 --> 00:14:15.930
and just asking questions

00:14:15.930 --> 00:14:19.680
about how these components
of states are encoded,

00:14:19.680 --> 00:14:22.620
like what makes a state persist?

00:14:22.620 --> 00:14:26.310
What gives a state a positive
or a negative valance?

00:14:26.310 --> 00:14:28.560
How do you crank up or crank down

00:14:28.560 --> 00:14:30.240
the intensity of the state?

00:14:30.240 --> 00:14:32.850
It just opens up a
whole bunch of questions

00:14:32.850 --> 00:14:35.130
that you can ask in the brain

00:14:35.130 --> 00:14:37.143
with the kinds of tools we have now.

00:14:38.460 --> 00:14:40.110
- You mentioned arousal a few times,

00:14:40.110 --> 00:14:41.463
and you mentioned valence,

00:14:42.660 --> 00:14:45.660
realizing that there are
these other aspects of states,

00:14:45.660 --> 00:14:46.890
I'd like to just talk about

00:14:46.890 --> 00:14:48.450
arousal a little bit more, and valence,

00:14:48.450 --> 00:14:50.250
because at a very basic level,

00:14:50.250 --> 00:14:52.410
it seems to me that arousal,

00:14:52.410 --> 00:14:55.863
we can be very alert and pissed off,

00:14:56.730 --> 00:15:01.140
stressed, worried, we can have insomnia,

00:15:01.140 --> 00:15:03.480
we can also be very
alert and be quite happy.

00:15:03.480 --> 00:15:08.340
So the valence flips, people
can be sexually aroused,

00:15:08.340 --> 00:15:11.190
people can be aroused
in all sorts of ways.

00:15:11.190 --> 00:15:16.190
Is there any simple or simple-ish
neurochemical signature

00:15:16.440 --> 00:15:17.970
that can flip valence?

00:15:17.970 --> 00:15:20.790
So for instance, is there any
way that we can safely say

00:15:20.790 --> 00:15:25.680
that arousal with some
additional dopamine release

00:15:25.680 --> 00:15:28.200
is going to be of positive valence,

00:15:28.200 --> 00:15:31.590
and arousal with very low dopamine

00:15:31.590 --> 00:15:33.843
is going to be of negative valence?

00:15:34.800 --> 00:15:39.270
- I would be reluctant to say
that it's a chemical flip,

00:15:39.270 --> 00:15:41.520
I would say it's more likely to be

00:15:41.520 --> 00:15:43.200
a circuit flip.
- Mm-hmm.

00:15:43.200 --> 00:15:44.787
- Different circuits being engaged.

00:15:44.787 --> 00:15:48.960
And it might be that a given
neurochemical, even dopamine,

00:15:48.960 --> 00:15:52.650
is involved in both
positively valanced arousal

00:15:52.650 --> 00:15:55.020
and negatively valanced arousal,

00:15:55.020 --> 00:15:59.280
that's why people think about
these as different axes.

00:15:59.280 --> 00:16:04.280
So I think the interesting
question that you touch on is,

00:16:04.560 --> 00:16:07.950
is arousal something that
is just completely generic

00:16:07.950 --> 00:16:09.000
in the brain,

00:16:09.000 --> 00:16:12.960
or are there actually
different kinds of arousal

00:16:12.960 --> 00:16:15.330
that are specific to different behaviors?

00:16:15.330 --> 00:16:16.410
And you raise the question,

00:16:16.410 --> 00:16:18.690
sexual arousal feels different

00:16:18.690 --> 00:16:21.510
from aggressive arousal, for example,

00:16:21.510 --> 00:16:24.480
and we actually published a paper on this,

00:16:24.480 --> 00:16:27.660
back in 2009, in fruit flies,

00:16:27.660 --> 00:16:29.910
where we found some evidence

00:16:29.910 --> 00:16:32.820
for two types of arousal states.

00:16:32.820 --> 00:16:35.640
One of which is sleep-wake arousal,

00:16:35.640 --> 00:16:37.380
you're more aroused when you wake up

00:16:37.380 --> 00:16:38.610
than when you're asleep,

00:16:38.610 --> 00:16:39.900
and flies show that,

00:16:39.900 --> 00:16:43.680
and the other is a startle response,

00:16:43.680 --> 00:16:46.740
an arousal response to
a mechanical stimulus,

00:16:46.740 --> 00:16:48.780
and not just mechanical stimulus.

00:16:48.780 --> 00:16:50.730
If you puff air on flies,

00:16:50.730 --> 00:16:53.490
kind of like trying to swat the wasp away

00:16:53.490 --> 00:16:55.620
from your burger at a picnic table,

00:16:55.620 --> 00:16:58.800
they come back more and
more and more vigorously.

00:16:58.800 --> 00:17:01.200
And we were able to dissect this

00:17:01.200 --> 00:17:05.190
and show that although both
of those forms of arousal

00:17:05.190 --> 00:17:09.300
required dopamine, they were exerted

00:17:09.300 --> 00:17:13.470
through completely separable
neural circuits in the fly.

00:17:13.470 --> 00:17:16.080
And so that really put, number one,

00:17:16.080 --> 00:17:18.210
the emphasis on it's the circuit

00:17:18.210 --> 00:17:20.640
that determines the type of arousal,

00:17:20.640 --> 00:17:24.090
but also that arousal isn't unitary,

00:17:24.090 --> 00:17:27.390
that there are behavior-specific
forms of arousal.

00:17:27.390 --> 00:17:29.790
And I think the jury is still out

00:17:29.790 --> 00:17:32.370
as to whether there is such a thing

00:17:32.370 --> 00:17:35.727
as completely generalized arousal or not.

00:17:35.727 --> 00:17:38.430
And I think some people
would argue there is,

00:17:38.430 --> 00:17:42.060
but I think more attention needs
to be paid to this question

00:17:42.060 --> 00:17:46.590
of domain-specific or
behavior-specific forms of arousal.

00:17:46.590 --> 00:17:48.180
- Yeah, it's a super-interesting idea,

00:17:48.180 --> 00:17:50.220
'cause I always thought of
arousal as along a continuum,

00:17:50.220 --> 00:17:52.020
like you can either be in a panic attack

00:17:52.020 --> 00:17:53.130
at the one end of the extreme,

00:17:53.130 --> 00:17:54.270
or you can be in a coma,

00:17:54.270 --> 00:17:56.700
and then somewhere in the
middle, you're alert and calm,

00:17:56.700 --> 00:18:00.570
but then this issue of
valence really, as you say,

00:18:00.570 --> 00:18:01.650
presents this opportunity

00:18:01.650 --> 00:18:04.570
that really there might be
multiple circuits for arousal.

00:18:04.570 --> 00:18:07.080
- Yeah.
- Or multiple mechanisms

00:18:07.080 --> 00:18:09.330
that would include
neurochemicals, as well as

00:18:09.330 --> 00:18:11.220
different neural pathways.
- Yeah.

00:18:11.220 --> 00:18:15.090
- So like to talk a bit about a state,

00:18:15.090 --> 00:18:18.030
if it is indeed a state,
which is aggression,

00:18:18.030 --> 00:18:20.490
your labs worked extensively on this.

00:18:20.490 --> 00:18:22.890
And if you would, could you highlight

00:18:22.890 --> 00:18:25.020
some of the key findings there,

00:18:25.020 --> 00:18:27.540
which brain areas that are involved?

00:18:27.540 --> 00:18:30.190
The beautiful work of Dayu
Lin and others in your lab

00:18:32.040 --> 00:18:33.600
that point to the idea

00:18:33.600 --> 00:18:37.140
that indeed there are kind
of switches in the brain,

00:18:37.140 --> 00:18:40.260
but that thinking of
switches for aggression

00:18:40.260 --> 00:18:42.540
might be too simple,

00:18:42.540 --> 00:18:44.370
how should we think about aggression?

00:18:44.370 --> 00:18:47.190
And I'll just sort of skew the question

00:18:47.190 --> 00:18:48.780
a bit more by saying,

00:18:48.780 --> 00:18:51.660
we see lots of different
kinds of aggression,

00:18:51.660 --> 00:18:56.310
this terrible school shooting
down in Texas recently,

00:18:56.310 --> 00:18:58.290
clearly an act that included aggression,

00:18:58.290 --> 00:19:00.150
and yet, you could imagine

00:19:00.150 --> 00:19:01.350
that's a very different type of aggression

00:19:01.350 --> 00:19:05.490
than an all-out rage or
a controlled aggression,

00:19:05.490 --> 00:19:06.660
there's a lot of variation there.

00:19:06.660 --> 00:19:09.030
So what are your thoughts on aggression,

00:19:09.030 --> 00:19:10.980
how it's generated, the
neural circuit mechanisms

00:19:10.980 --> 00:19:13.800
and some of the variation
in what we call aggression?

00:19:13.800 --> 00:19:15.300
- Yeah, this is a great question,

00:19:15.300 --> 00:19:17.670
and it's a large area.

00:19:17.670 --> 00:19:20.373
I would say that, first of all,

00:19:21.300 --> 00:19:24.750
the word aggression, in my mind,

00:19:24.750 --> 00:19:28.320
refers more to a description of behavior

00:19:28.320 --> 00:19:31.233
than it does to an internal state.

00:19:32.340 --> 00:19:35.280
Aggression could reflect an internal state

00:19:35.280 --> 00:19:38.250
that we would call anger in humans,

00:19:38.250 --> 00:19:40.560
or could reflect fear,

00:19:40.560 --> 00:19:45.057
or it could reflect hunger
if it's predatory aggression.

00:19:45.057 --> 00:19:47.670
And so this gets at the
issue that you raised

00:19:47.670 --> 00:19:51.390
of the different types
of aggression that exist.

00:19:51.390 --> 00:19:55.680
The work that Dayu did
when she was in my lab

00:19:55.680 --> 00:19:58.710
that really broke open the field

00:19:58.710 --> 00:20:01.740
to the application of modern genetic tools

00:20:01.740 --> 00:20:03.990
for studying circuits in mice

00:20:03.990 --> 00:20:08.990
is that she found a way to
evoke aggression in mice

00:20:09.900 --> 00:20:14.900
using optogenetics to
activate specific neurons

00:20:15.450 --> 00:20:18.330
in a region of the hypothalamus,

00:20:18.330 --> 00:20:21.300
the ventromedial hypothalamus, VMH,

00:20:21.300 --> 00:20:23.340
which people had been studying

00:20:23.340 --> 00:20:27.420
and looking at for decades,

00:20:27.420 --> 00:20:29.733
following, first, the work of,

00:20:31.110 --> 00:20:34.740
in cats, the famous
Nobel Prize-winning work

00:20:34.740 --> 00:20:36.450
of Walter Hess,

00:20:36.450 --> 00:20:38.370
and then followed by work

00:20:38.370 --> 00:20:42.570
done by Menno Kruk, in
the Netherlands, in rats,

00:20:42.570 --> 00:20:46.920
where they would stick
electrical wires into the brain

00:20:46.920 --> 00:20:49.590
and send electric currents into the brain,

00:20:49.590 --> 00:20:52.800
and they could trigger a placid cat

00:20:52.800 --> 00:20:56.040
to suddenly bare its teeth, hiss

00:20:56.040 --> 00:20:58.473
and almost strike out at the experimenter,

00:20:59.370 --> 00:21:03.450
and they could trigger rats
to fight with each other.

00:21:03.450 --> 00:21:07.170
And even in Hess's original experiments,

00:21:07.170 --> 00:21:09.960
he describes two types of aggression

00:21:09.960 --> 00:21:11.910
that he evokes from cats

00:21:11.910 --> 00:21:14.610
depending on where in the hypothalamus

00:21:14.610 --> 00:21:16.830
he puts his electrode,

00:21:16.830 --> 00:21:20.010
one of which he calls defensive rage,

00:21:20.010 --> 00:21:24.330
that's the ears laid back,
teeth bared and hissing.

00:21:24.330 --> 00:21:27.540
And the other one is predatory aggression,

00:21:27.540 --> 00:21:30.630
where the cat has its ears forward,

00:21:30.630 --> 00:21:34.380
and it's batting with its
paw at a mouse-like object,

00:21:34.380 --> 00:21:36.780
like it wants to catch it and eat it.

00:21:36.780 --> 00:21:39.300
So he already had, at that stage,

00:21:39.300 --> 00:21:42.780
some information about
segregation in the brain

00:21:42.780 --> 00:21:44.760
of different forms of aggression.

00:21:44.760 --> 00:21:48.560
So fast forward to 2008, 2009,

00:21:52.980 --> 00:21:54.750
when Dayu came to the lab

00:21:54.750 --> 00:21:58.140
and we had started working
on aggression in fruit flies,

00:21:58.140 --> 00:22:00.810
and I wanted to bring it into mice

00:22:00.810 --> 00:22:03.210
so that we could apply genetic tools.

00:22:03.210 --> 00:22:05.460
And we started by having Dayu,

00:22:05.460 --> 00:22:08.130
who was an electrophysiologist,

00:22:08.130 --> 00:22:11.760
just repeat the electrical stimulation

00:22:11.760 --> 00:22:15.210
of the ventromedial
hypothalamus in the mouse,

00:22:15.210 --> 00:22:17.760
just like people had done in rats,

00:22:17.760 --> 00:22:20.580
in cats, in hamsters, even in monkeys.

00:22:20.580 --> 00:22:23.520
And she could not get
that experiment to work

00:22:23.520 --> 00:22:26.370
over 40 different trials,

00:22:26.370 --> 00:22:27.510
it just didn't work.

00:22:27.510 --> 00:22:31.080
What she got instead was fear behaviors,

00:22:31.080 --> 00:22:35.760
she got freezing, cornering and crouching.

00:22:35.760 --> 00:22:39.090
And finally, in desperation,

00:22:39.090 --> 00:22:41.760
and we got a lot of input
from Menno Kruk on this,

00:22:41.760 --> 00:22:42.997
he really was mystified,

00:22:42.997 --> 00:22:44.820
"Why doesn't it work in mice?"

00:22:44.820 --> 00:22:47.670
We realized why there had been no paper

00:22:47.670 --> 00:22:52.440
on brain-stimulated aggression
in mice in 50 years,

00:22:52.440 --> 00:22:54.870
'cause the experiment doesn't work.

00:22:54.870 --> 00:22:59.190
And the one bit of
credit I can claim there

00:22:59.190 --> 00:23:01.953
is I convinced Dayu to try optogenetics,

00:23:03.390 --> 00:23:08.390
because it just had sort of
come into use deep in the brain,

00:23:09.750 --> 00:23:12.063
from Karl Deisseroth and others' work.

00:23:13.110 --> 00:23:15.450
And I thought maybe because it could be

00:23:15.450 --> 00:23:19.350
directed more specifically
to a region of the brain

00:23:19.350 --> 00:23:24.350
and types of cells than
electrical stimulation,

00:23:24.660 --> 00:23:25.500
it might work.

00:23:25.500 --> 00:23:27.840
And Dayu said, "Never,
never going to work.

00:23:27.840 --> 00:23:29.580
If it doesn't work with electricity,

00:23:29.580 --> 00:23:32.670
why should it work with optogenetics?"

00:23:32.670 --> 00:23:35.430
And the fact is that it did work,

00:23:35.430 --> 00:23:40.140
and we were able to trigger
aggression in this region

00:23:40.140 --> 00:23:44.010
using optogenetic stimulation
of ventromedial hypothalamus.

00:23:44.010 --> 00:23:46.590
And in retrospect, I think the reason

00:23:46.590 --> 00:23:50.280
that we were seeing all
these fear behaviors

00:23:50.280 --> 00:23:53.730
is because right at the upper part,

00:23:53.730 --> 00:23:56.700
if you think of ventromedial hypothalamus

00:23:56.700 --> 00:23:59.250
like a pear sitting on the ground,

00:23:59.250 --> 00:24:01.950
the fat part of the pear near the ground

00:24:01.950 --> 00:24:03.840
is where the aggression neurons are,

00:24:03.840 --> 00:24:07.470
but the upper part of the
pear has fear neurons.

00:24:07.470 --> 00:24:11.100
And it could be because
it's so small in a mouse,

00:24:11.100 --> 00:24:15.120
when you inject electrical
current anywhere in the pear,

00:24:15.120 --> 00:24:17.610
it flows up through the entire pear

00:24:17.610 --> 00:24:19.740
and it activates the fear circuits,

00:24:19.740 --> 00:24:22.770
and those totally dominate aggression.

00:24:22.770 --> 00:24:25.500
And so that's why we
were never able to see

00:24:25.500 --> 00:24:28.020
any fighting with electrical stimulation,

00:24:28.020 --> 00:24:29.700
whereas when you use optogenetics,

00:24:29.700 --> 00:24:34.080
you confine the stimulation
just to the region

00:24:34.080 --> 00:24:38.460
where you've implanted
the channelrhodopsin gene

00:24:38.460 --> 00:24:39.903
into those neurons.

00:24:41.130 --> 00:24:43.560
And so fast forward from that,

00:24:43.560 --> 00:24:47.790
from a lot of work from
Dayu now on her own at NYU,

00:24:47.790 --> 00:24:50.433
and with her postdoc, Annegret Falkner,

00:24:51.840 --> 00:24:53.550
as well as work of other people,

00:24:53.550 --> 00:24:56.890
there's evidence that the type of fighting

00:24:58.320 --> 00:25:01.770
that we elicit when we stimulate VMH

00:25:01.770 --> 00:25:04.410
is offensive aggression

00:25:04.410 --> 00:25:07.800
that is actually rewarding to male mice.

00:25:07.800 --> 00:25:09.300
- They like it.
- They like it,

00:25:09.300 --> 00:25:13.200
male mice will learn to poke their nose

00:25:13.200 --> 00:25:16.350
or press a bar to get the opportunity

00:25:16.350 --> 00:25:19.980
to beat up a subordinate male mouse.

00:25:19.980 --> 00:25:22.230
And in more recent experiments,

00:25:22.230 --> 00:25:24.600
if you activate those neurons

00:25:24.600 --> 00:25:26.460
and the mouse has a chance to be

00:25:26.460 --> 00:25:29.070
in one of two compartments in a box,

00:25:29.070 --> 00:25:31.230
they will gravitate
towards the compartment

00:25:31.230 --> 00:25:33.420
where those neurons are activated,

00:25:33.420 --> 00:25:35.940
it has a positive valance.

00:25:35.940 --> 00:25:37.740
And when I went into this field

00:25:37.740 --> 00:25:41.220
and I was thinking, "Well,
what goes on in my brain

00:25:41.220 --> 00:25:43.530
and my body when I'm furious?"

00:25:43.530 --> 00:25:47.460
it certainly doesn't feel
like a rewarding experience,

00:25:47.460 --> 00:25:50.130
it's not something that
I would want to repeat

00:25:50.130 --> 00:25:52.620
because it feels good
when I'm in that state,

00:25:52.620 --> 00:25:55.560
it doesn't feel good at
all when I'm in that state.

00:25:55.560 --> 00:25:59.250
And it is still, I think, a mystery

00:25:59.250 --> 00:26:01.260
as to where that type of aggression,

00:26:01.260 --> 00:26:03.810
which is more defensive aggression,

00:26:03.810 --> 00:26:07.050
the kind of aggression you
feel if you're being attacked

00:26:07.050 --> 00:26:09.990
or if you've been cheated by somebody,

00:26:09.990 --> 00:26:14.130
where that is encoded in the
brain and how that works,

00:26:14.130 --> 00:26:17.430
still, I think, is a
very important mystery

00:26:17.430 --> 00:26:18.930
that we haven't solved.

00:26:18.930 --> 00:26:22.230
And predatory aggression there
has been some progress on,

00:26:22.230 --> 00:26:24.510
so mice show predatory aggression,

00:26:24.510 --> 00:26:27.210
they use that to catch
crickets that they eat,

00:26:27.210 --> 00:26:29.730
and that involves different circuits

00:26:29.730 --> 00:26:32.730
than the ventromedial
hypothalamic circuits.

00:26:32.730 --> 00:26:36.660
So it's become clear that,
if you want to call it

00:26:36.660 --> 00:26:41.660
the state of aggressiveness,
is multifaceted,

00:26:41.820 --> 00:26:44.550
it depends on the type of aggression

00:26:44.550 --> 00:26:47.613
and it involves different
sorts of circuits.

00:26:48.720 --> 00:26:50.340
There's a paper suggesting

00:26:50.340 --> 00:26:54.720
that there might be a final common pathway

00:26:54.720 --> 00:26:57.720
for all aggression in a region,

00:26:57.720 --> 00:26:59.190
which is one of my favorites,

00:26:59.190 --> 00:27:01.770
it's called the substantia innominata,

00:27:01.770 --> 00:27:04.470
the substance with no name, I like.

00:27:04.470 --> 00:27:06.525
- Anatomists are so creative.
- Yes.

00:27:06.525 --> 00:27:08.700
[Andrew laughing]
Or the nucleus ambiguous,

00:27:08.700 --> 00:27:11.310
or the zona incerta, these are places

00:27:11.310 --> 00:27:13.110
that no one can think of what they are.

00:27:13.110 --> 00:27:16.080
Anyhow, that might be
a final common pathway

00:27:16.080 --> 00:27:17.880
for predatory aggression,

00:27:17.880 --> 00:27:20.400
and offensive and defensive aggression,

00:27:20.400 --> 00:27:22.590
but it can be really hard to tell

00:27:22.590 --> 00:27:25.470
just from looking at a mouse fight

00:27:25.470 --> 00:27:29.640
whether it's engaged in offensive
or defensive aggression.

00:27:29.640 --> 00:27:31.470
We've tried to take that apart

00:27:31.470 --> 00:27:34.590
using machine learning
analysis of behavior,

00:27:34.590 --> 00:27:36.300
but in rats, for example,

00:27:36.300 --> 00:27:38.700
it's much clearer when the animal

00:27:38.700 --> 00:27:41.820
is engaged in offensive
versus defensive aggression.

00:27:41.820 --> 00:27:43.530
They direct their bites

00:27:43.530 --> 00:27:46.020
at different parts of the opponent's body.

00:27:46.020 --> 00:27:47.430
- [Andrew] In particular.

00:27:47.430 --> 00:27:51.210
- Offensive aggression is flank directed,

00:27:51.210 --> 00:27:53.820
defensive aggression goes for the neck,

00:27:53.820 --> 00:27:55.170
goes for the throat.
- Mm,

00:27:55.170 --> 00:27:56.460
I've seen some nature specials

00:27:56.460 --> 00:27:59.129
where in a very barbaric way,

00:27:59.129 --> 00:28:00.840
[laughing] at least to me, it seems,

00:28:00.840 --> 00:28:05.160
like hyenas will try and go
after the reproductive axis,

00:28:05.160 --> 00:28:07.380
they'll go after testicles and penis

00:28:07.380 --> 00:28:10.950
and they basically want to,
it seems they want to limit

00:28:10.950 --> 00:28:13.697
future breeding potential.
- Yes, or create pain.

00:28:13.697 --> 00:28:14.850
[David laughing]
- Right, or create pain,

00:28:14.850 --> 00:28:15.720
or both.
- Yeah.

00:28:15.720 --> 00:28:18.810
- Yeah, I mean, in terms
of offensive aggression

00:28:18.810 --> 00:28:23.344
and your reflection that
it doesn't feel good,

00:28:23.344 --> 00:28:25.410
I mean, I can say, I know some people

00:28:25.410 --> 00:28:27.150
who really enjoy fighting.

00:28:27.150 --> 00:28:28.080
- [David] Hmm.

00:28:28.080 --> 00:28:29.490
- I have a relative who's a lawyer,

00:28:29.490 --> 00:28:31.320
he loves to argue and fight.

00:28:31.320 --> 00:28:32.160
- [David] Huh.

00:28:32.160 --> 00:28:34.710
- I don't think of him
as physically aggressive,

00:28:34.710 --> 00:28:35.580
in fact, he's not,

00:28:35.580 --> 00:28:38.580
but loves to fight and loves to prosecute

00:28:38.580 --> 00:28:39.840
and go after people.
- Hmm.

00:28:39.840 --> 00:28:41.380
- And he's pretty effective at it.

00:28:41.380 --> 00:28:42.270
- Right.
- I have a friend,

00:28:42.270 --> 00:28:44.490
former military special operations,

00:28:44.490 --> 00:28:47.230
and very calm guy, had a great career

00:28:48.480 --> 00:28:49.800
in military special operations,

00:28:49.800 --> 00:28:52.710
and he'll quite plainly
say, "I love to fight."

00:28:52.710 --> 00:28:54.390
- Mm-hmm.
- "It's one my great joys."

00:28:54.390 --> 00:28:55.710
He really enjoyed his work.

00:28:55.710 --> 00:28:56.970
- [David] Yep.

00:28:56.970 --> 00:28:58.860
- And also respected the other side

00:28:58.860 --> 00:29:01.830
because they offered the
opportunity to test that

00:29:01.830 --> 00:29:03.060
and to experience that joy.

00:29:03.060 --> 00:29:04.980
So in a kind of bizarre
way to somebody like me,

00:29:04.980 --> 00:29:08.320
who I'll certainly defend
my stance if I need to.

00:29:08.320 --> 00:29:10.020
- Yeah.
- But I certainly

00:29:10.020 --> 00:29:11.100
don't consider myself somebody

00:29:11.100 --> 00:29:13.800
who offensively goes after
people just to go after them,

00:29:13.800 --> 00:29:17.190
there's no, quote-unquote,
dopamine hit here.

00:29:17.190 --> 00:29:18.210
- Right.
- Acknowledging that dopamine

00:29:18.210 --> 00:29:19.410
does many things, of course.

00:29:19.410 --> 00:29:21.300
- Yeah.
- I have couple of questions

00:29:21.300 --> 00:29:24.000
about the way you describe the circuitry,

00:29:24.000 --> 00:29:25.800
I should say, the way the
circuitry is arranged.

00:29:25.800 --> 00:29:26.880
- [David] Mm-hmm.

00:29:26.880 --> 00:29:28.020
- And of course, we don't know,

00:29:28.020 --> 00:29:31.436
because we weren't consulted
at the design phase.

00:29:31.436 --> 00:29:33.930
[David laughing]
But why do you think

00:29:33.930 --> 00:29:36.870
there would be such a close
positioning of neurons

00:29:36.870 --> 00:29:41.250
that can elicit such divergent
states and behaviors?

00:29:41.250 --> 00:29:43.530
I mean, you're talking about
this pear-shaped structure,

00:29:43.530 --> 00:29:46.050
where the neurons that generate fear

00:29:46.050 --> 00:29:48.300
are cheek to jowl with the neurons

00:29:48.300 --> 00:29:51.630
that generate offensive
aggression of all things.

00:29:51.630 --> 00:29:55.890
It's like putting the neurons
that control swallowing

00:29:55.890 --> 00:29:58.230
next to the neurons that control vomiting,

00:29:58.230 --> 00:30:00.210
[laughing] it just seems to me that,

00:30:00.210 --> 00:30:01.440
on the one hand, this is the way

00:30:01.440 --> 00:30:03.030
that neural circuits are often arranged,

00:30:03.030 --> 00:30:04.590
and yet, to me, it's
always been perplexing

00:30:04.590 --> 00:30:06.270
as to why this would be the case.

00:30:06.270 --> 00:30:10.170
- Yeah, I think that is
a very profound question,

00:30:10.170 --> 00:30:14.133
and I've wondered about that a lot.

00:30:15.210 --> 00:30:19.083
If you think from an
evolutionary perspective,

00:30:20.280 --> 00:30:22.440
it might have been the case

00:30:22.440 --> 00:30:26.340
that defensive behaviors and fear

00:30:26.340 --> 00:30:30.633
arose before offensive aggression,

00:30:31.530 --> 00:30:33.810
because animals, first and foremost,

00:30:33.810 --> 00:30:38.160
have to defend themselves from
predation by other animals.

00:30:38.160 --> 00:30:41.250
And maybe it's only
when they're comfortable

00:30:41.250 --> 00:30:43.590
with having warded off predation

00:30:43.590 --> 00:30:45.270
and made themselves safe,

00:30:45.270 --> 00:30:47.797
that they can start to think about,

00:30:47.797 --> 00:30:51.840
"Who's going to be the alpha
male in my group here?"

00:30:51.840 --> 00:30:56.370
And so it could be that if
you think that brain regions

00:30:56.370 --> 00:31:00.210
and cell populations evolve by duplication

00:31:00.210 --> 00:31:05.210
and modification of
preexisting cell populations,

00:31:05.400 --> 00:31:08.670
that might be the way that those regions

00:31:08.670 --> 00:31:10.713
wound up next to each other.

00:31:11.580 --> 00:31:14.310
And developmentally, they start out

00:31:14.310 --> 00:31:16.740
from a common pool of precursors

00:31:16.740 --> 00:31:18.540
that expresses the same gene,

00:31:18.540 --> 00:31:21.270
the fear neurons and
the aggression neurons,

00:31:21.270 --> 00:31:22.560
and then with development,

00:31:22.560 --> 00:31:24.990
it gets shut off in the aggression neurons

00:31:24.990 --> 00:31:27.630
and maintained in the fear neurons.

00:31:27.630 --> 00:31:30.990
Now, that view says, "Oh, it's an accident

00:31:30.990 --> 00:31:32.640
of evolution and development,"

00:31:32.640 --> 00:31:36.120
but I think there must be
a functional part as well.

00:31:36.120 --> 00:31:38.880
So one thing we know
about offensive aggression

00:31:38.880 --> 00:31:42.480
is that strong fear shuts it down,

00:31:42.480 --> 00:31:46.140
whereas defensive
aggression, at least in rats,

00:31:46.140 --> 00:31:48.750
is actually enhanced by fear.

00:31:48.750 --> 00:31:50.460
It's one of the big differences

00:31:50.460 --> 00:31:54.270
between defensive aggression
and offensive aggression.

00:31:54.270 --> 00:31:55.920
And if you think about it,

00:31:55.920 --> 00:31:59.250
if offensive aggression is
rewarding and pleasurable,

00:31:59.250 --> 00:32:01.650
if you start to get really scared,

00:32:01.650 --> 00:32:03.900
that tends to take the fun out of it,

00:32:03.900 --> 00:32:07.110
and maybe these two regions
are close to each other

00:32:07.110 --> 00:32:12.110
to facilitate inhibition of
aggression by the fear neurons.

00:32:13.350 --> 00:32:16.560
We know for a fact that if
we deliberately stimulate

00:32:16.560 --> 00:32:19.380
those fear neurons at the top of the pear,

00:32:19.380 --> 00:32:21.480
when two animals are involved in a fight,

00:32:21.480 --> 00:32:24.300
it just stops the fight dead in its tracks

00:32:24.300 --> 00:32:27.030
and they go off into
the corner and freeze.

00:32:27.030 --> 00:32:30.810
So at least hierarchically,
it seems like fear

00:32:30.810 --> 00:32:34.350
is the dominant behavior
over offensive aggression,

00:32:34.350 --> 00:32:37.320
and how that inhibition
would work is not clear,

00:32:37.320 --> 00:32:39.960
'cause all these neurons
are pretty much excitatory,

00:32:39.960 --> 00:32:41.910
they're almost all glutamatergic.

00:32:41.910 --> 00:32:45.840
And so one of the interesting
questions for the future is,

00:32:45.840 --> 00:32:49.260
how exactly does fear dominate over

00:32:49.260 --> 00:32:52.680
and shut down offensive
aggression in the brain?

00:32:52.680 --> 00:32:54.750
How does that work, is it all circuitry,

00:32:54.750 --> 00:32:56.520
are there chemicals involved?

00:32:56.520 --> 00:33:00.300
What's the mechanism and
when is it called into play?

00:33:00.300 --> 00:33:02.280
But I think that's the way

00:33:02.280 --> 00:33:04.290
I tend to think about why these neurons

00:33:04.290 --> 00:33:06.360
are all mixed up together.

00:33:06.360 --> 00:33:09.570
And it's not just fight and freezing,

00:33:09.570 --> 00:33:10.500
or fight and flight,

00:33:10.500 --> 00:33:14.430
there are also metabolic
neurons that are mixed together

00:33:14.430 --> 00:33:16.080
in VMH as well.
- Mm-hmm.

00:33:16.080 --> 00:33:18.360
Controlling body-wide metabolism?

00:33:18.360 --> 00:33:19.278
- Yeah.

00:33:19.278 --> 00:33:20.250
- Very interesting.
- There are neurons there

00:33:20.250 --> 00:33:21.750
that respond to glucose,

00:33:21.750 --> 00:33:25.770
when glucose goes up in your
bloodstream, they're activated,

00:33:25.770 --> 00:33:30.150
and VMH has a whole history
in the field of obesity,

00:33:30.150 --> 00:33:35.150
because if you destroy it
in a rat, you get a fat rat.

00:33:35.220 --> 00:33:38.150
So the way most of the
world thinks about VMH

00:33:38.150 --> 00:33:40.260
is they think about, "Oh, that's the thing

00:33:40.260 --> 00:33:42.120
that keeps you from getting fat."

00:33:42.120 --> 00:33:44.160
It's the anti-obesity area,

00:33:44.160 --> 00:33:47.190
but in the area of social behavior,

00:33:47.190 --> 00:33:50.700
we see it as a center
for control of aggression

00:33:50.700 --> 00:33:51.810
and fear behaviors.

00:33:51.810 --> 00:33:56.400
And again, why these
neurons and these functions,

00:33:56.400 --> 00:33:58.410
I like to call them the four Fs,

00:33:58.410 --> 00:34:00.926
feeding, freezing, fighting and mating.

00:34:00.926 --> 00:34:01.950
- Mm-hmm.
- That they all seem to be

00:34:01.950 --> 00:34:04.050
closely intermingled with each other,

00:34:04.050 --> 00:34:06.480
maybe because crosstalk between them

00:34:06.480 --> 00:34:09.540
is very important to
help the animal's brain

00:34:09.540 --> 00:34:12.840
decide what behavior to prioritize

00:34:12.840 --> 00:34:16.263
and what behavior to shut
down at any given moment.

00:34:17.190 --> 00:34:18.420
- One of the things that we will do

00:34:18.420 --> 00:34:22.380
is link to the incredible
videos of these mice

00:34:22.380 --> 00:34:26.443
that have selective stimulation
of neurons in the VMH,

00:34:26.443 --> 00:34:28.743
Dayu's and the other
studies that you've done.

00:34:29.670 --> 00:34:33.510
Whenever I teach, I show
those videos at some point,

00:34:33.510 --> 00:34:36.570
with the caveats and
warnings that are required

00:34:36.570 --> 00:34:40.830
when one is about to
see a video of a mouse

00:34:40.830 --> 00:34:42.060
trying to mate with another mouse,

00:34:42.060 --> 00:34:43.410
or mating with another mouse,

00:34:43.410 --> 00:34:45.150
and they seem both to be quite happy

00:34:45.150 --> 00:34:46.530
about the mating experience,

00:34:46.530 --> 00:34:49.083
at least as far as we
know, as observers of mice.

00:34:50.190 --> 00:34:55.190
And then upon stimulation
of those VMH neurons,

00:34:55.470 --> 00:34:58.890
one of the mice essentially
tries to kill the other mouse.

00:34:58.890 --> 00:35:02.760
And then when that stimulation is stopped,

00:35:02.760 --> 00:35:04.650
they basically go back to hanging out.

00:35:04.650 --> 00:35:06.243
They don't go right back to mating.

00:35:06.243 --> 00:35:07.290
- Right.
- There's some reconciliation

00:35:07.290 --> 00:35:08.790
clearly that needs to
[laughing] happen first,

00:35:08.790 --> 00:35:09.840
we assume.
- Yes.

00:35:09.840 --> 00:35:10.797
- But it's just so striking,

00:35:10.797 --> 00:35:12.720
and I think equally striking is the video

00:35:12.720 --> 00:35:15.933
where the mouse is alone
in there with the glove,

00:35:16.770 --> 00:35:18.330
the VMH neurons are stimulated

00:35:18.330 --> 00:35:20.160
and the mouse goes into a rage,

00:35:20.160 --> 00:35:21.090
it looks like it wants to kill

00:35:21.090 --> 00:35:22.980
the glove, basically.
- Yep.

00:35:22.980 --> 00:35:25.530
- So striking, I encourage
people to go watch those,

00:35:25.530 --> 00:35:29.700
because it really puts a
tremendous amount of color

00:35:29.700 --> 00:35:30.840
on what we're describing,

00:35:30.840 --> 00:35:34.920
and it's just the idea that
there are switches in the brain,

00:35:34.920 --> 00:35:37.440
to me, really became
clear upon seeing that.

00:35:37.440 --> 00:35:40.260
One of the, excuse me, one of the concepts

00:35:40.260 --> 00:35:43.140
that you've raised in your lectures before

00:35:43.140 --> 00:35:45.300
and that I think was Hess's idea

00:35:45.300 --> 00:35:47.430
is this idea of a sort
of hydraulic pressure.

00:35:47.430 --> 00:35:49.770
- Mm-hmm.
- Or maybe it was Konrad,

00:35:49.770 --> 00:35:50.750
I can't speak now.

00:35:50.750 --> 00:35:53.100
[David laughing]
Excuse me, Konrad Lorenz,

00:35:53.100 --> 00:35:53.933
pardon.
- Mm-hmm.

00:35:53.933 --> 00:35:55.680
- Who talked about a kind
of hydraulic pressure

00:35:55.680 --> 00:35:57.210
towards behavior.

00:35:57.210 --> 00:36:00.150
I'm fascinated by this
idea of hydraulic pressure,

00:36:00.150 --> 00:36:03.180
because I don't consider
myself a hot-tempered person,

00:36:03.180 --> 00:36:06.390
but I am familiar with the fact
that when I lose my temper,

00:36:06.390 --> 00:36:09.690
it takes quite a while
for me to simmer down.

00:36:09.690 --> 00:36:11.250
I can't think about anything else,

00:36:11.250 --> 00:36:12.660
I don't want to think about anything else.

00:36:12.660 --> 00:36:15.360
In fact, trying to think
about anything else

00:36:15.360 --> 00:36:17.370
becomes aversive to me,

00:36:17.370 --> 00:36:21.930
which, to me, underscores
this notion of prioritization

00:36:21.930 --> 00:36:23.580
of the different states.
- Mm-hmm.

00:36:23.580 --> 00:36:25.430
- And potentially conflicting states.

00:36:26.760 --> 00:36:28.560
What do you think funnels into this idea

00:36:28.560 --> 00:36:31.170
of hydraulic pressure toward a state?

00:36:31.170 --> 00:36:36.120
And why is it, perhaps, that
sometimes we can be very angry,

00:36:36.120 --> 00:36:39.750
and if we succeed in winning an argument,

00:36:39.750 --> 00:36:41.910
all of a sudden, it will subside?

00:36:41.910 --> 00:36:42.840
Because clearly that means

00:36:42.840 --> 00:36:44.310
that there are external influences,

00:36:44.310 --> 00:36:46.170
it's a complex space
here that we're creating,

00:36:46.170 --> 00:36:47.003
I realize I'm creating

00:36:47.003 --> 00:36:47.836
a bit of a cloud.
- Yeah.

00:36:47.836 --> 00:36:49.860
- And I'm doing it on
purpose, because, to me,

00:36:49.860 --> 00:36:51.780
the idea of a hydraulic
pressure towards a state,

00:36:51.780 --> 00:36:53.610
like sleep, there's a sleep pressure.

00:36:53.610 --> 00:36:54.750
- Yeah.
- There is a pressure

00:36:54.750 --> 00:36:56.430
towards aggression, that all makes sense,

00:36:56.430 --> 00:36:58.020
but what's involved?

00:36:58.020 --> 00:36:59.670
Is it too multifactorial

00:36:59.670 --> 00:37:02.640
to actually separate out the variables,

00:37:02.640 --> 00:37:06.510
but what's really driving
hydraulic pressure

00:37:06.510 --> 00:37:08.010
toward a given state?

00:37:08.010 --> 00:37:11.940
- Yeah, so really important question,

00:37:11.940 --> 00:37:15.390
I think one way that is
helpful, at least for me,

00:37:15.390 --> 00:37:18.390
to break this question
apart and think about it

00:37:18.390 --> 00:37:22.920
is to distinguish homeostatic behaviors,

00:37:22.920 --> 00:37:25.200
that is, need-based behaviors,

00:37:25.200 --> 00:37:29.400
where the pressure is
built up because of a need,

00:37:29.400 --> 00:37:32.160
like, "I'm hungry, I need to eat.

00:37:32.160 --> 00:37:35.100
I'm thirsty, I need to drink.

00:37:35.100 --> 00:37:38.100
I'm hot, I need to get to a cold place,"

00:37:38.100 --> 00:37:41.640
it's basically the thermostat
model of your brain.

00:37:41.640 --> 00:37:42.930
You have a set point,

00:37:42.930 --> 00:37:44.760
and then if the temperature gets too hot,

00:37:44.760 --> 00:37:46.080
you turn on the AC,

00:37:46.080 --> 00:37:47.910
and if the temperature gets too cold,

00:37:47.910 --> 00:37:48.870
you turn on the heater

00:37:48.870 --> 00:37:51.090
and you put yourself
back to the set point.

00:37:51.090 --> 00:37:53.730
I don't think that's how aggression works.

00:37:53.730 --> 00:37:56.760
That is, it's not that we all go around,

00:37:56.760 --> 00:37:57.900
at least subjectively,

00:37:57.900 --> 00:38:02.640
I don't go around with an
accumulating need to fight,

00:38:02.640 --> 00:38:07.170
which I then look for
an excuse to release it.

00:38:07.170 --> 00:38:08.667
Now, maybe there are people that do that,

00:38:08.667 --> 00:38:12.314
and they go out and look for
bar fights to get into to.

00:38:12.314 --> 00:38:13.360
- Or Twitter.

00:38:13.360 --> 00:38:14.528
- Yeah, [laughing] or Twitter,

00:38:14.528 --> 00:38:15.361
yeah.
- Twitter seems to,

00:38:15.361 --> 00:38:18.270
I'm sort of half joking,
because Twitter seems to draw

00:38:18.270 --> 00:38:20.730
a reasonably sized crowd of people

