00:39:46.860 --> 00:39:51.860
- So you can think of this
accumulated hydraulic pressure

00:39:52.140 --> 00:39:55.560
either being based on something
that you were deprived of

00:39:55.560 --> 00:39:57.930
creating an accumulating need,

00:39:57.930 --> 00:40:00.540
or something that you want to do

00:40:00.540 --> 00:40:04.920
building up a drive or
a pressure to do that,

00:40:04.920 --> 00:40:08.970
and the natural way to think
about that, at least for me,

00:40:08.970 --> 00:40:13.500
is as gradual increases in neural activity

00:40:13.500 --> 00:40:15.930
in a particular region of the brain.

00:40:15.930 --> 00:40:19.890
And so for example, in the
area of the hypothalamus

00:40:19.890 --> 00:40:21.390
that controls feeding,

00:40:21.390 --> 00:40:23.880
Scott Sternson and others have shown

00:40:23.880 --> 00:40:26.220
that the hungrier you get,

00:40:26.220 --> 00:40:28.560
the higher the level of activity

00:40:28.560 --> 00:40:30.300
in that region in the brain,

00:40:30.300 --> 00:40:31.920
and then when you eat, boom,

00:40:31.920 --> 00:40:34.560
the activity goes right back down again.

00:40:34.560 --> 00:40:38.580
And that state is actually
negatively valenced,

00:40:38.580 --> 00:40:41.790
so it's like the animal, quote-unquote,

00:40:41.790 --> 00:40:44.550
feels increasingly uncomfortable,

00:40:44.550 --> 00:40:47.190
just like we feel
increasingly uncomfortable

00:40:47.190 --> 00:40:48.600
the hungrier we are,

00:40:48.600 --> 00:40:51.000
and then when we eat, it tamps it down,

00:40:51.000 --> 00:40:53.340
but there is this increased activity.

00:40:53.340 --> 00:40:55.470
And I think in the case of aggression,

00:40:55.470 --> 00:40:57.090
our data and others show

00:40:57.090 --> 00:41:00.390
that the more strongly you drive

00:41:00.390 --> 00:41:03.630
this region of the brain optogenetically,

00:41:03.630 --> 00:41:07.260
the more of just a hair trigger you need

00:41:07.260 --> 00:41:09.963
to set the animal off to get it to fight.

00:41:09.963 --> 00:41:11.730
Now, the interesting thing

00:41:11.730 --> 00:41:15.780
is that if there is nothing
for the animal to attack,

00:41:15.780 --> 00:41:17.550
it doesn't really do much

00:41:17.550 --> 00:41:19.650
when you're stimulating this region.

00:41:19.650 --> 00:41:23.160
It sort of wanders around
the cage a little bit more,

00:41:23.160 --> 00:41:27.690
but it will not actually show overt attack

00:41:27.690 --> 00:41:29.640
unless you put something in front of it.

00:41:29.640 --> 00:41:31.530
And the same thing is true for the areas

00:41:31.530 --> 00:41:34.470
we've described that
control mating behavior.

00:41:34.470 --> 00:41:36.390
This is what Lindsay is working on,

00:41:36.390 --> 00:41:39.990
you can stimulate those areas
'til you're blue in the face,

00:41:39.990 --> 00:41:42.180
and the mouse just sort of wanders around,

00:41:42.180 --> 00:41:44.490
but if you put another mouse in,

00:41:44.490 --> 00:41:46.800
wham, he will try to mount that mouse,

00:41:46.800 --> 00:41:48.992
if you put a kumquat in the cage.

00:41:48.992 --> 00:41:49.950
[Andrew laughing]
He'll try to mount

00:41:49.950 --> 00:41:51.210
the kumquat,

00:41:51.210 --> 00:41:54.840
and so it becomes a sort
of any port in the storm.

00:41:54.840 --> 00:41:58.530
So there is this idea that the drive

00:41:58.530 --> 00:42:00.780
is building up pressure

00:42:00.780 --> 00:42:03.570
that somehow needs to be released

00:42:03.570 --> 00:42:07.650
where that pressure is
actually being exerted,

00:42:07.650 --> 00:42:10.260
if you accept that it's increased activity

00:42:10.260 --> 00:42:12.900
in some circuit or circuits someplace,

00:42:12.900 --> 00:42:17.900
what is it pushing up against
that needs something else

00:42:18.750 --> 00:42:22.860
to sort of unplug it in
the Lorenz hydraulic model?

00:42:22.860 --> 00:42:25.050
That is, you don't see the behavior

00:42:25.050 --> 00:42:27.900
until you release a valve on this bucket

00:42:27.900 --> 00:42:31.410
and let the accumulated pressure flow out.

00:42:31.410 --> 00:42:33.930
And that's one of the
things we're trying to study

00:42:33.930 --> 00:42:37.410
in the context of the
mating behavior as well,

00:42:37.410 --> 00:42:39.240
how does the information

00:42:39.240 --> 00:42:41.520
that there's an object in front of you

00:42:41.520 --> 00:42:44.040
come together with this drive state

00:42:44.040 --> 00:42:46.320
that is generated by stimulating

00:42:46.320 --> 00:42:48.577
these neurons in the hypothalamus to say,

00:42:48.577 --> 00:42:50.820
"Okay, pull the trigger and go,

00:42:50.820 --> 00:42:53.340
it's time to mate, it's time to attack?"

00:42:53.340 --> 00:42:56.820
And we're just starting to get
some insights into that now.

00:42:56.820 --> 00:42:59.700
- Fascinating, and I
should mention to people,

00:42:59.700 --> 00:43:00.990
Dr. Anderson mentioned Lindsay,

00:43:00.990 --> 00:43:02.610
Lindsay is a former
graduate student of mine

00:43:02.610 --> 00:43:04.440
that's now a postdoc in David's lab.

00:43:04.440 --> 00:43:06.330
And I haven't caught up with her recently

00:43:06.330 --> 00:43:07.407
to hear about these experiments,

00:43:07.407 --> 00:43:09.030
but they sound fascinating.

