00:45:07.620 --> 00:45:09.540
iI it wandering around
really wanting to mate

00:45:09.540 --> 00:45:10.650
and really wanting to fight?

00:45:10.650 --> 00:45:12.123
We, of course, don't know,

00:45:13.110 --> 00:45:15.360
but is its heart rate up,

00:45:15.360 --> 00:45:17.250
is its blood pressure up?

00:45:17.250 --> 00:45:19.532
Is it wishing that there was pornography?

00:45:19.532 --> 00:45:21.630
[David and Andrew laughing]

00:45:21.630 --> 00:45:23.970
Something's going on, presumably,

00:45:23.970 --> 00:45:28.970
that's different than
prior to that stimulation,

00:45:28.980 --> 00:45:31.230
and is it arousal?

00:45:31.230 --> 00:45:32.550
And what do you think it is

00:45:32.550 --> 00:45:37.143
about the visual factory
perception of a conspecific

00:45:37.143 --> 00:45:40.380
that ungates this tremendous
repertoire of behaviors?

00:45:40.380 --> 00:45:43.830
- Right, that is a central question.

00:45:43.830 --> 00:45:47.730
I can say, at least with
respect to the fear neurons

00:45:47.730 --> 00:45:49.740
that sit on top of the aggression neurons,

00:45:49.740 --> 00:45:51.510
we know that when those neurons

00:45:51.510 --> 00:45:54.360
are activated optogenetically,

00:45:54.360 --> 00:45:57.660
in the same way we would
activate the aggression neurons,

00:45:57.660 --> 00:46:01.290
that there's clearly an arousal
process that's occurring,

00:46:01.290 --> 00:46:04.320
you can see the pupils
dilate in the animal.

00:46:04.320 --> 00:46:05.790
There is an increase

00:46:05.790 --> 00:46:09.090
in stress hormone release
into the bloodstream,

00:46:09.090 --> 00:46:12.510
we've shown that heart rate goes up.

00:46:12.510 --> 00:46:17.280
So in addition to the
drive to actually freeze,

00:46:17.280 --> 00:46:19.650
which is what those animals do,

00:46:19.650 --> 00:46:22.350
there is autonomic arousal

00:46:22.350 --> 00:46:26.190
and neuroendocrine activation
of stress responses.

00:46:26.190 --> 00:46:29.040
And some of that is probably shared

00:46:29.040 --> 00:46:32.190
by the aggression neurons
and the mating neurons,

00:46:32.190 --> 00:46:35.430
although we haven't investigated
it in as much detail,

00:46:35.430 --> 00:46:37.140
but I wouldn't be surprised

00:46:37.140 --> 00:46:40.920
because they project to
many of the same regions

00:46:40.920 --> 00:46:44.340
that the fear neurons project to,

00:46:44.340 --> 00:46:46.470
which is a interesting issue

00:46:46.470 --> 00:46:48.810
in the context to discuss later maybe,

00:46:48.810 --> 00:46:51.150
in the context of why we're comfortable

00:46:51.150 --> 00:46:53.280
with mental illnesses that are based

00:46:53.280 --> 00:46:56.610
on maladaptations of fear,
but not mental illnesses

00:46:56.610 --> 00:46:59.760
that are based on
maladaptations of aggression

00:46:59.760 --> 00:47:02.490
if they have pretty similar
circuits in the brain.

00:47:02.490 --> 00:47:05.610
But that's how I would imagine

00:47:05.610 --> 00:47:08.820
there is an arousal dimension, as you say,

00:47:08.820 --> 00:47:12.150
there are stress hormones that
are activated, these regions,

00:47:12.150 --> 00:47:16.770
VMH projects to about 30
different regions in the brain,

00:47:16.770 --> 00:47:20.040
and it gets input from
about 30 different regions.

00:47:20.040 --> 00:47:23.430
So I kind of see it as both an antenna

00:47:23.430 --> 00:47:25.170
and a broadcasting center,

00:47:25.170 --> 00:47:28.770
it's like a satellite dish
that takes in information

00:47:28.770 --> 00:47:31.290
from different sensory modalities,

00:47:31.290 --> 00:47:36.030
smell, maybe vision, mechanosensation,

00:47:36.030 --> 00:47:39.870
and then it sort of
synthesizes and integrates that

00:47:39.870 --> 00:47:43.050
into a fairly low-dimensional,

00:47:43.050 --> 00:47:45.660
as the computational people call it,

00:47:45.660 --> 00:47:48.810
representation of this pressure to attack,

00:47:48.810 --> 00:47:51.780
and it broadcasts that all over the brain

00:47:51.780 --> 00:47:53.700
to trigger all these systems

00:47:53.700 --> 00:47:55.770
that have to be brought into play

00:47:55.770 --> 00:47:58.530
if the animal is going
to engage in aggression.

00:47:58.530 --> 00:48:01.350
Because aggression is a very risky thing

00:48:01.350 --> 00:48:03.030
for an animal to engage in,

00:48:03.030 --> 00:48:04.680
it could wind up losing

00:48:04.680 --> 00:48:06.750
and it could wind up getting killed,

00:48:06.750 --> 00:48:10.290
and so its brain constantly has to make

00:48:10.290 --> 00:48:14.430
a cost-benefit analysis of
whether to continue on that path

00:48:14.430 --> 00:48:16.410
or to back off as well.

00:48:16.410 --> 00:48:19.590
And I think that part of
this broadcasting function

00:48:19.590 --> 00:48:24.540
of this region is engaging
all these other brain domains

00:48:24.540 --> 00:48:29.280
that play a role in this kind
of cost-benefit analysis.

00:48:29.280 --> 00:48:31.320
- I want to talk more
about mating behavior,

00:48:31.320 --> 00:48:33.630
but as a segue to that,

00:48:33.630 --> 00:48:37.020
as we're talking about
aggression and mating behavior,

00:48:37.020 --> 00:48:38.400
I think, "Hormones."

00:48:38.400 --> 00:48:41.010
And whenever there's an
opportunity on this podcast

00:48:41.010 --> 00:48:44.670
to shatter a common myth, I grab it.

00:48:44.670 --> 00:48:46.968
One of the common myths that's out there,

00:48:46.968 --> 00:48:48.180
and I think that persists,

00:48:48.180 --> 00:48:52.740
is that testosterone makes
animals and humans aggressive,

00:48:52.740 --> 00:48:56.820
and estrogen makes animals
placid and kind or emotional.

00:48:56.820 --> 00:48:58.200
And as we both know,

00:48:58.200 --> 00:48:59.940
nothing could be further from the truth,

00:48:59.940 --> 00:49:01.260
although there's some truth to the idea

00:49:01.260 --> 00:49:03.840
that these hormones are all involved.

00:49:03.840 --> 00:49:08.310
Robert Sapolsky supplied
some information to me

00:49:08.310 --> 00:49:09.720
when he came on this podcast,

00:49:09.720 --> 00:49:12.600
that if you give people
exogenous testosterone,

00:49:12.600 --> 00:49:15.510
it tends to make them more
of the way they were before.

00:49:15.510 --> 00:49:17.610
If they were a jerk before,
they'll become more of a jerk,

00:49:17.610 --> 00:49:18.930
if they were very altruistic,

00:49:18.930 --> 00:49:20.130
they'll become more altruistic.

00:49:20.130 --> 00:49:21.547
And then eventually I pointed out,

00:49:21.547 --> 00:49:23.820
"You'll aromatize that
testosterone into estrogen

00:49:23.820 --> 00:49:25.080
and you'll start getting
opposite effects,"

00:49:25.080 --> 00:49:27.990
so it's a murky space,
it's not straightforward.

00:49:27.990 --> 00:49:30.750
But if I'm not mistaken,

00:49:30.750 --> 00:49:33.450
testosterone plays a role
in generating aggression,

00:49:33.450 --> 00:49:37.530
however, the specific hormones

00:49:37.530 --> 00:49:40.720
that are involved in
generating aggression via VMH

00:49:41.910 --> 00:49:44.280
are things other than testosterone.

00:49:44.280 --> 00:49:45.570
Can you tell us a little
bit more about that?

00:49:45.570 --> 00:49:47.700
'Cause there's some
interesting surprises in there.

00:49:47.700 --> 00:49:50.190
- Yeah, that's a really
important question.

00:49:50.190 --> 00:49:55.190
So when we finally
identified the neurons in VMH

00:49:55.380 --> 00:49:58.800
that control aggression
with a molecular marker,

00:49:58.800 --> 00:50:02.640
we found out that that marker
was the estrogen receptor.

00:50:02.640 --> 00:50:05.070
So that might strike
you as a little strange,

00:50:05.070 --> 00:50:10.070
why should aggression-promoting
neurons in male mice

00:50:10.470 --> 00:50:13.500
be labeled with the estrogen receptor?

00:50:13.500 --> 00:50:17.280
Other labs have shown
that the estrogen receptor

00:50:17.280 --> 00:50:20.940
in adult male mice is
necessary for aggression.

00:50:20.940 --> 00:50:23.520
If you knock out the gene in VMH,

00:50:23.520 --> 00:50:24.900
they don't fight.

00:50:24.900 --> 00:50:27.360
And it's been shown, and
a lot of this is work

00:50:27.360 --> 00:50:29.880
from your colleague,
Nirao Shah, at Stanford,

00:50:29.880 --> 00:50:32.700
who is one of my former PhD students,

00:50:32.700 --> 00:50:35.580
that if you castrate a mouse

00:50:35.580 --> 00:50:39.180
and it loses the ability to fight,

00:50:39.180 --> 00:50:41.790
not only can you rescue fighting

00:50:41.790 --> 00:50:43.920
with a testosterone implant,

00:50:43.920 --> 00:50:46.740
but you can rescue it
with an estrogen implant.

00:50:46.740 --> 00:50:50.010
So you can bypass
completely the requirement

00:50:50.010 --> 00:50:54.090
for testosterone to restore
aggressiveness to the mice.

00:50:54.090 --> 00:50:54.923
And as you say,

00:50:54.923 --> 00:50:59.010
it's because many of the
effects of testosterone,

00:50:59.010 --> 00:51:01.590
although not all, many
of them are mediated

00:51:01.590 --> 00:51:04.590
by its conversion to estrogen,

00:51:04.590 --> 00:51:07.380
by a process called aromatization,

00:51:07.380 --> 00:51:10.920
it's carried out by an
enzyme called aromatase.

00:51:10.920 --> 00:51:13.560
In fact, most of your listeners

00:51:13.560 --> 00:51:15.630
may have heard of aromatase

00:51:15.630 --> 00:51:20.520
'cause aromatase inhibitors are
widely used in female humans

00:51:20.520 --> 00:51:23.550
as adjuvant chemotherapy
for breast cancer.

00:51:23.550 --> 00:51:28.550
They are a way of reducing
the production of estrogen

00:51:28.800 --> 00:51:30.960
by preventing testosterone

00:51:30.960 --> 00:51:33.120
from being converted into estrogen.

00:51:33.120 --> 00:51:35.340
And in fact, there are a
lot of animal experiments

00:51:35.340 --> 00:51:38.880
showing if you give males
aromatase inhibitors,

00:51:38.880 --> 00:51:40.230
they stop fighting,

00:51:40.230 --> 00:51:44.013
as well as also stop
being sexually active.

00:51:44.880 --> 00:51:48.450
And so that's one of the
counterintuitive ideas,

00:51:48.450 --> 00:51:51.450
and Nirao has shown that progesterone

00:51:51.450 --> 00:51:54.150
also seems to play a role in aggression,

00:51:54.150 --> 00:51:55.680
because these aggression neurons

00:51:55.680 --> 00:51:58.620
also express the progesterone receptor.

00:51:58.620 --> 00:52:02.130
So here are two hormones that
are classically thought of

00:52:02.130 --> 00:52:04.650
as female reproductive hormones,

00:52:04.650 --> 00:52:08.100
this is what goes up and goes
down during the estrous cycle,

00:52:08.100 --> 00:52:10.050
estrogen and progesterone,

00:52:10.050 --> 00:52:13.290
and yet, they're playing
a very important role

00:52:13.290 --> 00:52:17.160
in controlling aggression in male mice,

00:52:17.160 --> 00:52:19.950
and presumably in male humans as well.

00:52:19.950 --> 00:52:24.720
- Fascinating, so estrogen
is doing many more things

00:52:24.720 --> 00:52:25.710
than I think most people believe.

00:52:25.710 --> 00:52:28.200
- Yep.
- And testosterone is doing

00:52:28.200 --> 00:52:30.870
maybe different and fewer
things in some cases,

00:52:30.870 --> 00:52:32.103
and more in others.

00:52:33.540 --> 00:52:35.910
I've known some aggressive females

00:52:35.910 --> 00:52:38.430
over the time I've been alive,

00:52:38.430 --> 00:52:40.230
what's involved in female aggression

00:52:40.230 --> 00:52:42.420
that's unique from the pathways

00:52:42.420 --> 00:52:44.430
that generate male aggression?

00:52:44.430 --> 00:52:45.690
- Great question,

00:52:45.690 --> 00:52:50.580
so we and other labs have
studied this in both mice

00:52:50.580 --> 00:52:52.470
and also in fruit flies.

00:52:52.470 --> 00:52:56.850
So one thing in mice that
distinguishes aggression

00:52:56.850 --> 00:52:58.080
in females from males

00:52:58.080 --> 00:53:00.600
is that male mice are
pretty much ready to fight

00:53:00.600 --> 00:53:02.190
at the drop of a hat,

00:53:02.190 --> 00:53:07.190
female mice only fight
when they are nurturing

00:53:08.040 --> 00:53:10.110
and nursing their pups

00:53:10.110 --> 00:53:11.760
after they've delivered a litter.

00:53:11.760 --> 00:53:13.620
And there is a window there

00:53:13.620 --> 00:53:16.560
where they become hyper-aggressive,

00:53:16.560 --> 00:53:19.110
and then after their pups are weened,

00:53:19.110 --> 00:53:20.670
that aggressiveness goes away.

00:53:20.670 --> 00:53:23.160
So this is pretty remarkable

00:53:23.160 --> 00:53:25.920
that you take a virgin female mouse

00:53:25.920 --> 00:53:27.570
and expose it to a male,

00:53:27.570 --> 00:53:30.600
and her response is to
become sexually receptive

00:53:30.600 --> 00:53:32.010
and to mate with him.

00:53:32.010 --> 00:53:34.530
And now you let her have her pups,

00:53:34.530 --> 00:53:36.330
and you put the same male

00:53:36.330 --> 00:53:38.490
or another male mouse
in the cage with her,

00:53:38.490 --> 00:53:41.580
and instead of trying to mate
with him, she attacks him.

00:53:41.580 --> 00:53:44.880
So there is some presumably hormonal

00:53:44.880 --> 00:53:49.050
and also neuronal switch
that's occurring in the brain

00:53:49.050 --> 00:53:52.080
that switches the response of the female

00:53:52.080 --> 00:53:54.990
from sex to aggression

00:53:54.990 --> 00:53:57.600
when she goes from virginity to maternity.

00:53:57.600 --> 00:53:59.700
And we recently showed in a paper,

00:53:59.700 --> 00:54:03.090
this is work from one of
my students, Mengyu Liu,

00:54:03.090 --> 00:54:05.550
that within VMH in females,

00:54:05.550 --> 00:54:09.930
there are two clearly divisible subsets

00:54:09.930 --> 00:54:12.600
of estrogen receptor neurons,

00:54:12.600 --> 00:54:17.220
and she showed that one of
those subsets controls fighting

00:54:17.220 --> 00:54:19.350
and the other one controls mating.

00:54:19.350 --> 00:54:21.090
And in fact, if you stimulate

00:54:21.090 --> 00:54:24.900
the fighting-specific subset in a virgin,

00:54:24.900 --> 00:54:27.120
you can get the virgin to attack,

00:54:27.120 --> 00:54:31.200
which is something that we
were never able to do before,

00:54:31.200 --> 00:54:34.530
and if you stimulate the
mating one, you enhance mating.

00:54:34.530 --> 00:54:37.020
The reason we could
never get these results

00:54:37.020 --> 00:54:39.930
when we stimulated the whole population

00:54:39.930 --> 00:54:41.520
of estrogen receptor neurons

00:54:41.520 --> 00:54:45.270
is that these effects are
opposite and they cancel out.

00:54:45.270 --> 00:54:49.080
And so it turns out that
if you measure the activity

00:54:49.080 --> 00:54:51.330
of the fighting and the mating neurons

00:54:51.330 --> 00:54:55.200
going from a virgin to a maternal female,

00:54:55.200 --> 00:54:59.790
the aggression neurons are
very low in their activity

00:54:59.790 --> 00:55:01.140
in the virgin,

00:55:01.140 --> 00:55:04.110
but once the female has pups,

00:55:04.110 --> 00:55:08.760
the activation ability of
those neurons goes way up

00:55:08.760 --> 00:55:11.040
and the mating neurons stay the same.

00:55:11.040 --> 00:55:14.670
So if you think of the balance
between them like a seesaw,

00:55:14.670 --> 00:55:17.640
in the virgin, there is more activity

00:55:17.640 --> 00:55:20.400
in the mating neurons than
in the fighting neurons,

00:55:20.400 --> 00:55:23.010
whereas in the nursing mother,

00:55:23.010 --> 00:55:25.570
there's more activity or more activation

00:55:26.700 --> 00:55:27.630
the other way around,

00:55:27.630 --> 00:55:29.075
the fighting neurons in the mating.

00:55:29.075 --> 00:55:29.908
- Mm-hmm.
- Did I say,

00:55:29.908 --> 00:55:31.200
"Fighting and mating," the first?

00:55:31.200 --> 00:55:33.900
Mating neurons dominate
fighting in the virgin,

00:55:33.900 --> 00:55:37.470
fighting neurons dominate
mating in the mother.

00:55:37.470 --> 00:55:41.670
So that's a really cool observation,

00:55:41.670 --> 00:55:44.160
and it's not something
that happens in males,

00:55:44.160 --> 00:55:48.420
and we don't know what
causes that or controls that.

00:55:48.420 --> 00:55:52.620
Interestingly, this gets
into the whole issue

00:55:52.620 --> 00:55:57.150
of neurons that are present
in females, but not in males.

00:55:57.150 --> 00:56:00.540
So the field has known for a long time

00:56:00.540 --> 00:56:04.680
that male and female fruit
flies have sex-specific neurons.

00:56:04.680 --> 00:56:07.350
And most of the neurons
that we've identified

00:56:07.350 --> 00:56:10.380
in fruit flies that
control fighting in males

00:56:10.380 --> 00:56:11.730
are male specific,

00:56:11.730 --> 00:56:14.370
they're not found in the female brain,

00:56:14.370 --> 00:56:17.190
but recently, we discovered a set

00:56:17.190 --> 00:56:21.570
of female-specific fighting
neurons in the female brain,

00:56:21.570 --> 00:56:24.570
together with a couple
of other laboratories.

00:56:24.570 --> 00:56:28.860
Now, they do share one
common population of neurons

00:56:28.860 --> 00:56:31.200
in both male and female flies,

00:56:31.200 --> 00:56:32.610
that in females,

00:56:32.610 --> 00:56:35.340
activates the female-specific
fighting neurons,

00:56:35.340 --> 00:56:38.880
and in males, activates the
male-specific fighting neurons,

00:56:38.880 --> 00:56:42.690
so it's kind of a hierarchy
with this common neuron on top.

00:56:42.690 --> 00:56:45.360
And in mice, we discovered

00:56:45.360 --> 00:56:49.710
that there are male-specific
neurons in VMH,

00:56:49.710 --> 00:56:54.090
and those neurons are activated
during male aggression.

00:56:54.090 --> 00:56:57.000
Now, the neurons that
are active in females

00:56:57.000 --> 00:57:00.990
when females fight in
VMH are not sex specific,

00:57:00.990 --> 00:57:03.900
so they are also found in males.

00:57:03.900 --> 00:57:06.390
So this is already showing
you some complexity,

00:57:06.390 --> 00:57:08.730
the male mouse VMH

00:57:08.730 --> 00:57:11.640
has both male-specific aggression neurons

00:57:11.640 --> 00:57:13.860
and generic aggression neurons.

00:57:13.860 --> 00:57:15.660
And then the female VMH,

00:57:15.660 --> 00:57:18.690
the mating cells are
only found in females,

00:57:18.690 --> 00:57:20.280
they are female specific

00:57:20.280 --> 00:57:22.290
and not found in the male brain.

00:57:22.290 --> 00:57:23.850
And so we're trying to find out

00:57:23.850 --> 00:57:27.270
what these sex-specific
populations of neurons are doing,

00:57:27.270 --> 00:57:30.720
but that indicates that that
is some of the mechanism

00:57:30.720 --> 00:57:34.140
by which different sexes
show different behaviors.

00:57:34.140 --> 00:57:36.900
- I'm fixated on this transition

00:57:36.900 --> 00:57:38.370
from the virgin female mouse

00:57:38.370 --> 00:57:39.837
to the maternal female mouse,

00:57:39.837 --> 00:57:42.480
and I have a couple of
questions about whether or not,

00:57:42.480 --> 00:57:45.210
for instance, the transition is governed

00:57:45.210 --> 00:57:46.410
by the presence of pups.

00:57:46.410 --> 00:57:48.030
So for instance, if you
take a virgin female,

00:57:48.030 --> 00:57:49.233
she'll mate with a male,

00:57:50.100 --> 00:57:51.300
once she's had pups,

00:57:51.300 --> 00:57:54.810
she'll try and fight that
male, or presumably another

00:57:54.810 --> 00:57:56.250
intruder female, right?
- Yes,

00:57:56.250 --> 00:57:59.010
equally towards females
and male intruders.

00:57:59.010 --> 00:58:00.960
- Does that require the
presence of her pups?

00:58:00.960 --> 00:58:02.370
Meaning if you were to take those pups

00:58:02.370 --> 00:58:04.050
and give them to another mother,

00:58:04.050 --> 00:58:06.543
does she revert to the
more virgin-like behavior?

00:58:08.084 --> 00:58:09.150
Is it triggered by lactation

00:58:09.150 --> 00:58:10.950
or could it actually be triggered

00:58:10.950 --> 00:58:12.630
by the mating behavior itself?

00:58:12.630 --> 00:58:16.050
'Cause it's possible for the
virgin to become a non-virgin,

00:58:16.050 --> 00:58:18.210
but not actually have a litter of pups.

00:58:18.210 --> 00:58:20.580
- Right, those are all great questions,

00:58:20.580 --> 00:58:23.460
and we don't know the
answer to most of them.

00:58:23.460 --> 00:58:27.360
What I can say is that a nursing mother

00:58:27.360 --> 00:58:30.600
doesn't have to have her
pups with her in the cage

00:58:30.600 --> 00:58:33.630
in order to attack an intruder male

00:58:33.630 --> 00:58:35.403
or an intruder female,

00:58:36.420 --> 00:58:40.140
she is just in a state of brain

00:58:40.140 --> 00:58:43.380
that makes her aggressive to any intruder.

00:58:43.380 --> 00:58:47.160
And those aggression neurons
in that female's brain

00:58:47.160 --> 00:58:51.810
are activated by both male
and female intruders equally,

00:58:51.810 --> 00:58:54.870
whereas in male mice,
the aggression neurons

00:58:54.870 --> 00:58:59.100
are only ever activated
by males, not by females,

00:58:59.100 --> 00:59:02.340
because males are never
supposed to attack females,

00:59:02.340 --> 00:59:04.560
they're only supposed to mate with them.

00:59:04.560 --> 00:59:06.150
So that's another difference

00:59:06.150 --> 00:59:08.580
in how those neurons are tuned

00:59:08.580 --> 00:59:11.550
to signals from different conspecifics.

00:59:11.550 --> 00:59:13.830
Does it require lactation?

00:59:13.830 --> 00:59:15.390
I don't know the answer to that,

00:59:15.390 --> 00:59:17.280
I think there are some experiments

00:59:17.280 --> 00:59:20.400
where people have tried
to, classical experiments,

00:59:20.400 --> 00:59:24.510
people have tried to reproduce
the changes in hormones

00:59:24.510 --> 00:59:28.020
that occur during pregnancy in female rats

00:59:28.020 --> 00:59:30.150
to see if it can make them aggressive.

00:59:30.150 --> 00:59:33.810
And some of those manipulations
do, to some extent,

00:59:33.810 --> 00:59:36.090
but there's a whole biology there

00:59:36.090 --> 00:59:38.250
that remains to be explored

00:59:38.250 --> 00:59:40.650
about how much of this is hormones,

00:59:40.650 --> 00:59:43.830
how much of this is
circuitry and electricity,

00:59:43.830 --> 00:59:46.560
and how much of it is other factors

00:59:46.560 --> 00:59:48.630
that we haven't identified yet?

00:59:48.630 --> 00:59:50.040
- I don't want to anthropomorphize,

00:59:50.040 --> 00:59:52.860
but, well, I'll just ask the question.

00:59:52.860 --> 00:59:56.193
So the other day, I was
watching ferrets mate, right?

00:59:57.574 --> 01:00:00.570
They're mustelids, and
they're mating behavior,

01:00:00.570 --> 01:00:02.250
I guess I didn't say
why I was watching this,

01:00:02.250 --> 01:00:04.650
doesn't matter.
[David laughing]

01:00:04.650 --> 01:00:05.880
It simply doesn't matter,

01:00:05.880 --> 01:00:08.430
but if one observes the mating behaviors

01:00:08.430 --> 01:00:09.540
of different animals,

01:00:09.540 --> 01:00:11.280
we know that there's a tremendous range

01:00:11.280 --> 01:00:13.350
of mating behaviors in humans.

01:00:13.350 --> 01:00:15.450
There can be no aggressive component,

01:00:15.450 --> 01:00:16.340
there could be an aggressive component

01:00:16.340 --> 01:00:18.450
in humans that have all sorts of kinks

01:00:18.450 --> 01:00:19.770
and fetishes and behaviors,

01:00:19.770 --> 01:00:21.720
and most of which probably
has never been documented

01:00:21.720 --> 01:00:23.670
'cause most of this happens in private.

01:00:23.670 --> 01:00:24.960
And here, I always say on this podcast,

01:00:24.960 --> 01:00:27.480
any time we're talking about
sexual behavior in humans,

01:00:27.480 --> 01:00:28.740
we're always making the presumption

01:00:28.740 --> 01:00:31.290
that it's consensual, age appropriate,

01:00:31.290 --> 01:00:33.420
context appropriate and
species appropriate.

01:00:33.420 --> 01:00:35.520
Well, today, we're talking about
a lot of different species.

01:00:35.520 --> 01:00:38.940
With that said, just to set context,

01:00:38.940 --> 01:00:41.880
I was watching this
video of ferrets mating,

01:00:41.880 --> 01:00:44.490
and it's quite violent actually.

01:00:44.490 --> 01:00:45.930
There's a lot of neck biting,

01:00:45.930 --> 01:00:47.220
there's a lot of squealing.

01:00:47.220 --> 01:00:49.380
If I were going to project
and anthropomorphize,

01:00:49.380 --> 01:00:52.353
I'd say it's not really clear
they both want to be there,

01:00:53.730 --> 01:00:54.840
one would make that assumption.

01:00:54.840 --> 01:00:57.120
And of course, we don't
know, we have no idea,

01:00:57.120 --> 01:00:58.653
this could be the ritual.

01:01:00.330 --> 01:01:02.760
It seems, to me, that
there is some crossover

01:01:02.760 --> 01:01:06.480
of aggression and mating
behavior circuitry

01:01:06.480 --> 01:01:08.550
during the act of the mating,

01:01:08.550 --> 01:01:11.040
and do you think that reflects

01:01:11.040 --> 01:01:15.780
this sort of stew of competing neurons

01:01:15.780 --> 01:01:19.110
that are prioritizing in real time?

01:01:19.110 --> 01:01:21.570
Because, of course, as states,

01:01:21.570 --> 01:01:23.070
they have persistence, as you point out,

01:01:23.070 --> 01:01:25.320
and you can imagine
that states overlapping,

01:01:25.320 --> 01:01:26.250
four different states,

01:01:26.250 --> 01:01:27.750
the motivational drive to mate,

01:01:27.750 --> 01:01:30.330
the motivational drive to get
away from this experience,

01:01:30.330 --> 01:01:34.680
the motivational drive
to eat at some point,

01:01:34.680 --> 01:01:35.880
to defecate at some point,

01:01:35.880 --> 01:01:37.410
all of these things are competing,

01:01:37.410 --> 01:01:41.280
and what we're really seeing
is a bias in probabilities.

01:01:41.280 --> 01:01:44.100
But when you look at mating
behavior of various animals,

01:01:44.100 --> 01:01:47.760
you see an aggressive component
sometimes, but not always.

01:01:47.760 --> 01:01:51.270
Is it species specific,
is it context specific?

01:01:51.270 --> 01:01:54.150
And more generally, do
you think that there

01:01:54.150 --> 01:01:57.120
is crosstalk between these
different neuronal populations

01:01:57.120 --> 01:01:58.920
and the animal itself
might be kind of confused

01:01:58.920 --> 01:01:59.940
about what's going on?

01:01:59.940 --> 01:02:02.250
- Right, great questions.

01:02:02.250 --> 01:02:04.110
I can't really speak to the issue

01:02:04.110 --> 01:02:06.450
of whether this is species specific,

01:02:06.450 --> 01:02:09.180
'cause I'm not a
naturalist or a zoologist.

01:02:09.180 --> 01:02:10.800
I've seen, like you have,

01:02:10.800 --> 01:02:13.980
in the wild, for example,
lions when they mate,

01:02:13.980 --> 01:02:15.120
I've seen them in Africa,

01:02:15.120 --> 01:02:18.450
there's often a biting
component of that as well.

01:02:18.450 --> 01:02:20.580
One of the things that surprised us

01:02:20.580 --> 01:02:24.390
when we identified neurons in VMHvl

01:02:24.390 --> 01:02:27.210
that control aggression in males

01:02:27.210 --> 01:02:29.880
is that within that population,

01:02:29.880 --> 01:02:32.160
there is a subset of neurons

01:02:32.160 --> 01:02:34.800
that is activated by females

01:02:34.800 --> 01:02:37.320
during male-female mating encounters.

01:02:37.320 --> 01:02:42.320
Now, you don't generally think
of mouse sex as rough sex,

01:02:43.350 --> 01:02:48.000
but there is a lot of what superficially

01:02:48.000 --> 01:02:50.490
looks like violent behavior sometimes,

01:02:50.490 --> 01:02:54.900
especially if the female
rejects the male and runs away.

01:02:54.900 --> 01:02:57.390
And there's some evidence

01:02:57.390 --> 01:03:01.980
that those female-selective neurons in VMH

01:03:01.980 --> 01:03:06.450
are part of the mating behavior.

01:03:06.450 --> 01:03:08.310
If you shut 'em down,

01:03:08.310 --> 01:03:11.250
the animals don't mate as effectively

01:03:11.250 --> 01:03:13.230
as they otherwise would.

01:03:13.230 --> 01:03:16.500
What happens when you stimulate
them we don't yet know

01:03:16.500 --> 01:03:19.560
because we don't have a
way to specifically do that

01:03:19.560 --> 01:03:22.800
without activating the
male aggression neurons.

01:03:22.800 --> 01:03:26.400
But I think they must
be there for a reason

01:03:26.400 --> 01:03:30.390
because VMH is not
traditionally the brain region

01:03:30.390 --> 01:03:34.080
to which male sexual
behavior has been assigned.

01:03:34.080 --> 01:03:37.980
That's another area called
the medial preoptic area,

01:03:37.980 --> 01:03:41.070
and there we have shown
that there are neurons

01:03:41.070 --> 01:03:44.070
that definitely stimulate mating behavior.

01:03:44.070 --> 01:03:47.610
In fact, if we activate those
mating neurons in a male

01:03:47.610 --> 01:03:50.730
while it's in the middle
of attacking another male,

01:03:50.730 --> 01:03:54.450
it will stop fighting,
start singing to that male

01:03:54.450 --> 01:03:56.970
and start to try to mount that male

01:03:56.970 --> 01:03:58.860
until we shut those neurons off.

01:03:58.860 --> 01:04:02.220
So those are the
make-love-not-war neurons,

01:04:02.220 --> 01:04:05.400
and VMH are the make-war-not-love neurons,

01:04:05.400 --> 01:04:08.130
and there are dense interconnections

01:04:08.130 --> 01:04:10.470
between these two nuclei,

01:04:10.470 --> 01:04:13.860
which are very close to
each other in the brain.

01:04:13.860 --> 01:04:16.320
And we've shown that
some of those connections

01:04:16.320 --> 01:04:18.660
are mutually inhibitory,

01:04:18.660 --> 01:04:22.860
to prevent the animal
from attacking a mate

01:04:22.860 --> 01:04:24.720
that it's supposed to be mating with,

01:04:24.720 --> 01:04:28.470
or to prevent it from
mating with an animal

01:04:28.470 --> 01:04:29.940
it's supposed to be attacking.

01:04:29.940 --> 01:04:31.590
But it's also possible

01:04:31.590 --> 01:04:34.650
that there are some
cooperative interactions

01:04:34.650 --> 01:04:36.210
between those structures,

01:04:36.210 --> 01:04:39.930
as well as antagonistic interactions,

01:04:39.930 --> 01:04:42.870
and the balance of whether
it's the cooperative

01:04:42.870 --> 01:04:45.090
or antagonistic interactions

01:04:45.090 --> 01:04:47.490
that are firing at any given moment

01:04:47.490 --> 01:04:50.340
in a mating encounter, as you suggest,

01:04:50.340 --> 01:04:55.340
may determine whether a
moment of coital bliss

01:04:57.060 --> 01:05:01.770
among two lions may
suddenly turn into a snap

01:05:01.770 --> 01:05:04.440
or a growl and a baring of fangs.

01:05:04.440 --> 01:05:05.550
We don't know that,

01:05:05.550 --> 01:05:09.420
but certainly the substrate,
the wiring is there

01:05:09.420 --> 01:05:10.950
for that to happen.

01:05:10.950 --> 01:05:14.070
- I'm sure people's minds are
running wild with all this.

01:05:14.070 --> 01:05:15.900
I'll just use this as an opportunity

01:05:15.900 --> 01:05:18.180
to raise something I've wondered about

01:05:18.180 --> 01:05:19.830
for far too long, [laughing]

01:05:19.830 --> 01:05:23.220
which is, I have a friend
who's a psychiatrist

01:05:23.220 --> 01:05:25.560
who works on the treatment of fetishes.

01:05:25.560 --> 01:05:27.660
This is not a psychiatrist
that I was treated by,

01:05:27.660 --> 01:05:29.250
I'll just point that out.
[David laughing]

01:05:29.250 --> 01:05:30.930
But they mentioned something

01:05:30.930 --> 01:05:32.760
very interesting to me long ago,

01:05:32.760 --> 01:05:36.150
which is that when you
look at true fetishes,

01:05:36.150 --> 01:05:38.190
and what meets the criteria for fetish,

01:05:38.190 --> 01:05:41.040
that there does seem to be some,

01:05:41.040 --> 01:05:43.770
what one would think would
be competing circuitry

01:05:43.770 --> 01:05:46.950
that suddenly becomes aligned.

01:05:46.950 --> 01:05:51.950
For instance, avoidance of
feces, dead bodies, feet,

01:05:52.800 --> 01:05:55.110
things that are very infectious,

01:05:55.110 --> 01:05:57.930
typically those states of disgust

01:05:57.930 --> 01:06:00.510
are antagonistic to the states of desire,

01:06:00.510 --> 01:06:05.510
as one would hope is present
during sexual behavior.

01:06:05.730 --> 01:06:09.660
Fetishes often involve
exactly those things

01:06:09.660 --> 01:06:14.660
that are aversive, feet, dead bodies,

01:06:15.060 --> 01:06:17.520
disgusting things to most people,

01:06:17.520 --> 01:06:20.103
and true fetishes, in
the pathologic sense,

01:06:21.240 --> 01:06:24.840
exist when people have,
basically, a requirement

01:06:24.840 --> 01:06:26.490
for thinking about or even the presence

01:06:26.490 --> 01:06:29.520
of those ordinarily disgusting things

01:06:29.520 --> 01:06:31.170
in order to become sexually aroused.

01:06:31.170 --> 01:06:32.670
- Hmm.
- As if the circuitry

01:06:32.670 --> 01:06:35.430
has crossed over, and the
statement that wrung in my mind

01:06:35.430 --> 01:06:39.510
was people don't develop
fetishes to mailboxes,

01:06:39.510 --> 01:06:44.250
or to the color red, or to
random objects and things,

01:06:44.250 --> 01:06:47.250
they develop fetishes to things
that are highly infectious

01:06:47.250 --> 01:06:50.213
and counter-reproductive
appetitive states.

01:06:50.213 --> 01:06:51.900
- Hmm.
- So I find that interesting,

01:06:51.900 --> 01:06:53.850
I don't know if you have
any reflections on that

01:06:53.850 --> 01:06:55.650
as to why that might be.

01:06:55.650 --> 01:06:56.820
I'm tempted to ask whether or not

01:06:56.820 --> 01:06:59.430
you've ever observed
fetish-like behavior in mice,

01:06:59.430 --> 01:07:01.410
but I find it fascinating

01:07:01.410 --> 01:07:02.910
that you have this area of the brain

01:07:02.910 --> 01:07:04.580
that's so highly concerned
with the hypothalamus,

01:07:04.580 --> 01:07:06.990
in which you have these
dense populations intermixed,

01:07:06.990 --> 01:07:11.070
and that the addition of a
forebrain, especially in humans,

01:07:11.070 --> 01:07:12.840
that can think and make decisions

01:07:12.840 --> 01:07:15.270
could in some ways facilitate

01:07:15.270 --> 01:07:16.950
the expression of these
primitive behaviors,

01:07:16.950 --> 01:07:18.510
but could also complicate

01:07:18.510 --> 01:07:20.550
the expression of primitive behaviors.

01:07:20.550 --> 01:07:22.680
- Right, I would agree.

01:07:22.680 --> 01:07:26.070
I think one way of looking at fetishes

01:07:26.070 --> 01:07:28.650
from a neurobiological standpoint

01:07:28.650 --> 01:07:33.650
is that they represent a kind
of appetitive conditioning

01:07:34.200 --> 01:07:39.200
where something that is
natively aversive or disgusting,

01:07:39.390 --> 01:07:44.390
by being repeatedly paired
with a rewarding experience,

01:07:44.820 --> 01:07:49.110
changes its valence, its sign

01:07:49.110 --> 01:07:54.110
so that now it somehow produces
the anticipation of reward

01:07:55.290 --> 01:07:57.840
the next time a person sees it.

01:07:57.840 --> 01:08:01.110
Now, I don't know that
literature in animals,

01:08:01.110 --> 01:08:03.840
so I don't know if you could condition

01:08:03.840 --> 01:08:06.450
a mouse to eat feces, for example,

01:08:06.450 --> 01:08:10.140
although there are animals
that are naturally coprophagic,

01:08:10.140 --> 01:08:14.640
and maybe mice do that
occasionally, I'm not sure.

01:08:14.640 --> 01:08:18.030
But that is one way to think about it,

01:08:18.030 --> 01:08:20.820
and that could certainly
involve in humans,

01:08:20.820 --> 01:08:23.310
the more recently evolved
arts of the brain,

01:08:23.310 --> 01:08:27.330
the cortex that is sort of orchestrating

01:08:27.330 --> 01:08:29.850
both what behaviors are happening

01:08:29.850 --> 01:08:33.300
and whether reward states are turning on

01:08:33.300 --> 01:08:37.020
in association with those
behaviors that are happening.

01:08:37.020 --> 01:08:41.190
And that's the part that
I think is difficult

01:08:41.190 --> 01:08:44.310
and challenging to study in a mouse,

01:08:44.310 --> 01:08:48.240
but certainly bears thinking about,

01:08:48.240 --> 01:08:50.790
because it's a really interesting,

01:08:50.790 --> 01:08:53.730
again, sort of counterintuitive aspect.

01:08:53.730 --> 01:08:54.960
Again, like rough sex,

01:08:54.960 --> 01:08:57.270
people that want to have fighting,

01:08:57.270 --> 01:08:58.920
or violence, or aggressiveness

01:08:58.920 --> 01:09:02.517
in order to be sexually
aroused, and fetishes.

01:09:02.517 --> 01:09:05.940
And in fact, when we made
that discovery initially,

01:09:05.940 --> 01:09:08.010
it raised the question in my mind

01:09:08.010 --> 01:09:13.010
whether some people that are
serial rapists, for example,

01:09:14.790 --> 01:09:16.770
and engage in sexual violence

01:09:16.770 --> 01:09:21.150
might, in some level, have
their wires crossed in some way,

01:09:21.150 --> 01:09:23.640
that these states that are supposed to be

01:09:23.640 --> 01:09:27.510
pretty much separated and
mutually antagonistic are not,

01:09:27.510 --> 01:09:31.470
and are actually more
rewarding and reinforcing.

01:09:31.470 --> 01:09:33.840
I think it's going to be a long time

01:09:33.840 --> 01:09:35.970
before we have figured it out,

01:09:35.970 --> 01:09:37.770
but when you think about it,

01:09:37.770 --> 01:09:40.110
there is no treatment

01:09:40.110 --> 01:09:44.070
that we have for a violent sexual offender

01:09:44.070 --> 01:09:46.560
that eliminates the violence,

01:09:46.560 --> 01:09:50.580
but not the sexual desire and sexual urge,

01:09:50.580 --> 01:09:54.780
whether it's physical castration
or chemical castration,

01:09:54.780 --> 01:09:57.240
it eliminates both.

01:09:57.240 --> 01:09:59.160
- Definitely an area that I think,

01:09:59.160 --> 01:10:00.810
well, human neuroscience in general

01:10:00.810 --> 01:10:03.420
needs a lot of tools, right?

01:10:03.420 --> 01:10:06.780
In terms of how to probe and
manipulate neural circuitry.

01:10:06.780 --> 01:10:09.690
I'd love to turn to this
area that you mentioned,

01:10:09.690 --> 01:10:12.000
the medial preoptic area.

01:10:12.000 --> 01:10:14.817
I'm fascinated by it, because
just as within the VMH,

01:10:14.817 --> 01:10:16.470
you have these neurons for mating

01:10:16.470 --> 01:10:19.110
and fighting, or aggression,

01:10:19.110 --> 01:10:21.630
my understanding is medial preoptic area

01:10:21.630 --> 01:10:24.060
contains neurons for mating,

01:10:24.060 --> 01:10:26.310
but also for temperature regulation.

01:10:26.310 --> 01:10:28.440
And perhaps I'm making
too much of a leap here,

01:10:28.440 --> 01:10:31.140
but I've always wondered
about this phrase, "In heat,"

01:10:32.040 --> 01:10:36.990
as certainly the menstrual and
or estrous cycle in females

01:10:36.990 --> 01:10:38.850
is related to changes in body temperature.

01:10:38.850 --> 01:10:40.110
In fact, measuring body temperature

01:10:40.110 --> 01:10:43.260
is one way that women can fairly reliably

01:10:43.260 --> 01:10:44.793
predict ovulation, et cetera.

01:10:45.660 --> 01:10:48.360
Although this is not a
show about contraception,

01:10:48.360 --> 01:10:50.520
please rely on multiple methods
[laughing] as necessary,

01:10:50.520 --> 01:10:54.270
don't use this discussion as
your guide for contraception

01:10:54.270 --> 01:10:55.320
based on temperature.

01:10:55.320 --> 01:10:58.170
But if you stimulate certain neurons

01:10:58.170 --> 01:10:59.310
in the medial preoptic area,

01:10:59.310 --> 01:11:02.590
you can trigger dramatic
changes in body temperature

01:11:03.450 --> 01:11:06.120
and/or mating behavior.

01:11:06.120 --> 01:11:07.470
What's the relationship, if any,

01:11:07.470 --> 01:11:09.180
between temperature and mating,

01:11:09.180 --> 01:11:10.653
or do we simply not know?

01:11:11.610 --> 01:11:14.340
- I don't know what the relationship

01:11:14.340 --> 01:11:18.750
is between temperature and mating neurons

01:11:18.750 --> 01:11:20.940
in the preoptic area.

01:11:20.940 --> 01:11:25.320
I suspect that they are
different populations of neurons

01:11:25.320 --> 01:11:29.790
because it's become pretty
clear that the preoptic area

01:11:29.790 --> 01:11:32.550
has many different subsets of neurons

01:11:32.550 --> 01:11:36.150
that are specifically active
during different behaviors,

01:11:36.150 --> 01:11:38.760
even different phases of mating behavior.

01:11:38.760 --> 01:11:40.530
So there are mounting neurons,

01:11:40.530 --> 01:11:43.110
there are intromission thrusting neurons,

01:11:43.110 --> 01:11:46.020
and ejaculation neurons
and sniffing neurons.

01:11:46.020 --> 01:11:48.630
- Wait, wait, so I think
I've heard this before,

01:11:48.630 --> 01:11:49.887
but I just want to make
sure that people get this

01:11:49.887 --> 01:11:51.390
and I want to make sure I get this.

01:11:51.390 --> 01:11:55.530
So you're telling me within
medial preoptic area,

01:11:55.530 --> 01:11:57.780
there are specific neurons
that if you stimulate them,

01:11:57.780 --> 01:12:00.750
will make males thrust
as if they're mating?

01:12:00.750 --> 01:12:05.750
- No, so this is not based
on stimulation experiments.

01:12:05.880 --> 01:12:06.713
- Mm.
- It's based on

01:12:06.713 --> 01:12:08.910
imaging experiments right now.
- I see, I see.

01:12:08.910 --> 01:12:11.760
- That we see when we
look in the preoptic area

01:12:11.760 --> 01:12:14.100
at what neurons are active

01:12:14.100 --> 01:12:17.220
during different phases of aggression,

01:12:17.220 --> 01:12:19.260
we see that there are different neurons

01:12:19.260 --> 01:12:22.470
that are active during sniffing, mounting,

01:12:22.470 --> 01:12:24.870
thrusting and ejaculation,

01:12:24.870 --> 01:12:27.990
and they become repeatedly activated

01:12:27.990 --> 01:12:30.300
each time the animal
goes through that cycle.

01:12:30.300 --> 01:12:32.880
- During mating, yeah.
- During the mating cycle.

01:12:32.880 --> 01:12:34.380
There are also some neurons there

01:12:34.380 --> 01:12:37.350
that are active during
aggression, which are distinct,

01:12:37.350 --> 01:12:39.660
and we don't know whether those neurons

01:12:39.660 --> 01:12:42.210
are there to promote aggression

01:12:42.210 --> 01:12:46.230
or to inhibit mating when
animals are fighting.

01:12:46.230 --> 01:12:49.500
We have some evidence that
suggest it may be the latter,

01:12:49.500 --> 01:12:51.390
but we don't know for sure yet.

01:12:51.390 --> 01:12:54.240
The thermosensitive neurons
are really interesting,

01:12:54.240 --> 01:12:57.450
because you mentioned
the phrase, "In heat,"

01:12:57.450 --> 01:12:59.790
and then in the context of aggression,

01:12:59.790 --> 01:13:02.910
you talk about hotblooded
people or hotheads,

01:13:02.910 --> 01:13:04.410
there's just recently a paper

01:13:04.410 --> 01:13:08.880
showing there are thermoregulatory
neurons in VMH as well.

01:13:08.880 --> 01:13:11.760
So all of these homeostatic systems

01:13:11.760 --> 01:13:15.150
for metabolic control
and temperature control

01:13:15.150 --> 01:13:18.600
are intermingled in these nuclei,

01:13:18.600 --> 01:13:22.860
these zones that control these
basic survival behaviors,

01:13:22.860 --> 01:13:26.850
like mating and aggression
and predator defense.

01:13:26.850 --> 01:13:31.230
And I would imagine that
the thermal regulation

01:13:31.230 --> 01:13:35.550
is tightly connected
to energy expenditure,

01:13:35.550 --> 01:13:39.150
and that, again, these
neurons are mixed together

01:13:39.150 --> 01:13:43.020
to facilitate integration
of all these signals

01:13:43.020 --> 01:13:46.590
by the brain in some way
that we don't understand

01:13:46.590 --> 01:13:48.480
to maintain the proper balance

01:13:48.480 --> 01:13:53.480
between energy conservation
and energy consumption

01:13:53.580 --> 01:13:56.760
during this particular
behavior or that behavior.

01:13:56.760 --> 01:13:58.920
I mean, I've always been
fascinated by the question,

01:13:58.920 --> 01:14:03.000
why is it that violence
goes up in the summertime

01:14:03.000 --> 01:14:04.830
when the temperatures are high?

01:14:04.830 --> 01:14:07.200
Does it really have something to do

01:14:07.200 --> 01:14:12.060
with the idea that increased
temperature increases violence?

01:14:12.060 --> 01:14:15.330
It seems hard to believe
because we're homeothermic

01:14:15.330 --> 01:14:20.330
and we pretty much stay
around 98.6 Fahrenheit.

01:14:20.580 --> 01:14:23.610
It could be other social
reasons why that happens,

01:14:23.610 --> 01:14:25.560
people are outside, out on the street,

01:14:25.560 --> 01:14:27.030
bumping into each other,

01:14:27.030 --> 01:14:30.270
but I think there could well be something

01:14:30.270 --> 01:14:33.990
that ties thermoregulation
to aggressiveness,

01:14:33.990 --> 01:14:38.160
as well as to mating behavior.

01:14:38.160 --> 01:14:39.810
- Fascinating, yeah.

01:14:39.810 --> 01:14:43.350
I ask in the hopes that
maybe in the years to come

01:14:43.350 --> 01:14:46.380
your lab will parse some of
the temperature relationships.

01:14:46.380 --> 01:14:49.320
And I realize it could be also regulated

01:14:49.320 --> 01:14:50.430
by hormones in general,

01:14:50.430 --> 01:14:51.840
so it's tapping into two systems

01:14:51.840 --> 01:14:53.070
for completely different reasons,

01:14:53.070 --> 01:14:56.970
but anyway, an area that intrigues me,

01:14:56.970 --> 01:14:58.037
because of this notion of hotheadedness.

01:14:58.037 --> 01:15:00.300
- Right.
- Or cool, calm and collected.

01:15:00.300 --> 01:15:02.517
And also the fact that,

01:15:02.517 --> 01:15:04.350
and I probably should've
asked about this earlier,

01:15:04.350 --> 01:15:07.200
that arousal itself is tethered

01:15:07.200 --> 01:15:09.600
to the whole mating and
reproductive process.

01:15:09.600 --> 01:15:12.030
I mean, without a sort of seesawing back

01:15:12.030 --> 01:15:14.260
between the sympathetic
and parasympathetic

01:15:15.150 --> 01:15:17.310
arousal, relaxed states,

01:15:17.310 --> 01:15:19.503
there is no mating that will take place.

01:15:20.700 --> 01:15:21.990
So it's fascinating the way

01:15:21.990 --> 01:15:25.740
these different competing
forces and seesaws operate.

01:15:25.740 --> 01:15:27.900
Several times during
the discussion so far,

01:15:27.900 --> 01:15:30.600
we've hit on this idea

01:15:30.600 --> 01:15:34.740
that the same behavior can
reflect different states,

01:15:34.740 --> 01:15:36.450
and different states can converge

01:15:36.450 --> 01:15:39.330
on multiple behaviors as well.

01:15:39.330 --> 01:15:42.393
You had a paper not long
ago about mounting behavior,

01:15:43.290 --> 01:15:45.510
which I found fascinating.

01:15:45.510 --> 01:15:47.700
Maybe you could tell us about that result,

01:15:47.700 --> 01:15:49.170
because, to me, it really speaks

01:15:49.170 --> 01:15:51.750
to the fact that mounting behavior

01:15:51.750 --> 01:15:53.850
can, in one context, be sexual,

01:15:53.850 --> 01:15:56.430
and in another context,
actually be related to,

01:15:56.430 --> 01:15:57.930
we presume, dominance.

01:15:57.930 --> 01:16:02.103
And I think that my friends
who practice jujitsu,

01:16:03.780 --> 01:16:04.830
when I talk about that result,

01:16:04.830 --> 01:16:07.050
they say, "Of course,
mounting the other person

01:16:07.050 --> 01:16:08.820
and dominating them,

01:16:08.820 --> 01:16:10.200
there's nothing sexual about it,"

01:16:10.200 --> 01:16:12.390
it's about overtaking them physically,

01:16:12.390 --> 01:16:14.310
literally being on their neck side,

01:16:14.310 --> 01:16:17.550
as opposed to lying on their own back.

01:16:17.550 --> 01:16:18.383
- [David] Hmm.

01:16:18.383 --> 01:16:19.290
- Just fascinating,

01:16:19.290 --> 01:16:20.123
very primitive.
- Hmm.

01:16:20.123 --> 01:16:23.070
- And yet, I think speaks to this idea

01:16:23.070 --> 01:16:24.690
that mounting behavior might be

01:16:24.690 --> 01:16:26.430
one of the most fundamental ways

01:16:26.430 --> 01:16:28.800
in which animals and perhaps even humans

01:16:28.800 --> 01:16:33.800
express dominance and/or
sexual interactions.

01:16:34.020 --> 01:16:37.500
- Yep, and that's a fascinating question,

01:16:37.500 --> 01:16:39.840
and it was harder to figure out

01:16:39.840 --> 01:16:41.220
than you might've thought.

01:16:41.220 --> 01:16:44.640
So there's been this debate
for a long time in the field,

01:16:44.640 --> 01:16:48.690
when you see two male
mice mounting each other,

01:16:48.690 --> 01:16:50.850
is this homosexual behavior,

01:16:50.850 --> 01:16:54.270
is this a case of mistaken
sexual identification,

01:16:54.270 --> 01:16:56.400
or is this dominance behavior?

01:16:56.400 --> 01:16:59.520
And if you train an AI algorithm

01:16:59.520 --> 01:17:03.780
to try to distinguish male-male mounting

01:17:03.780 --> 01:17:05.340
from male-female mounting,

01:17:05.340 --> 01:17:07.140
it does not do a very good job,

01:17:07.140 --> 01:17:11.430
because motorically, those
behaviors look so similar.

01:17:11.430 --> 01:17:16.320
And so how did we wind up figuring out

01:17:16.320 --> 01:17:20.910
that most male-male mounting
is dominance mounting?

01:17:20.910 --> 01:17:24.030
There are two important clues,

01:17:24.030 --> 01:17:26.820
one is the context,

01:17:26.820 --> 01:17:29.280
and so male-male mounting

01:17:29.280 --> 01:17:32.370
tends to be more prominent among mice

01:17:32.370 --> 01:17:35.580
when they haven't had a
lot of fighting experience.

01:17:35.580 --> 01:17:40.410
And then as they become more
experienced in fighting,

01:17:40.410 --> 01:17:43.710
they will show relatively less mounting

01:17:43.710 --> 01:17:46.080
towards the other male and more attack,

01:17:46.080 --> 01:17:50.130
and they'll transition quickly
from mounting to attack,

01:17:50.130 --> 01:17:53.190
and so the mounting is always seen

01:17:53.190 --> 01:17:57.990
in this context of an overall
aggressive interaction.

01:17:57.990 --> 01:17:59.400
And then the second thing,

01:17:59.400 --> 01:18:01.380
which, believe it or not, was suggested

01:18:01.380 --> 01:18:05.460
by a computational,
theoretical person in my lab,

01:18:05.460 --> 01:18:09.150
Ann Kennedy, who now has
her own lab at Northwestern.

01:18:09.150 --> 01:18:12.480
She said, "Well, males are known to sing

01:18:12.480 --> 01:18:15.600
when they mount females,
ultrasonic vocalizations,

01:18:15.600 --> 01:18:18.960
why don't you see what kinds
of songs they're singing

01:18:18.960 --> 01:18:20.280
when they're mounting males?

01:18:20.280 --> 01:18:21.780
Maybe it's a different kind of song."

01:18:21.780 --> 01:18:22.950
Well, what we found out is,

01:18:22.950 --> 01:18:26.820
they don't sing at all when
they're mounting a male,

01:18:26.820 --> 01:18:29.550
so you can easily distinguish

01:18:29.550 --> 01:18:33.600
whether mounting behavior by a male mouse

01:18:33.600 --> 01:18:37.710
is reproductive or agonistic, aggressive,

01:18:37.710 --> 01:18:40.140
according to whether it's accompanied

01:18:40.140 --> 01:18:43.560
by ultrasonic vocalizations or not.

01:18:43.560 --> 01:18:47.040
And it turns out that
different brain regions

01:18:47.040 --> 01:18:49.050
are maximally active

01:18:49.050 --> 01:18:51.030
during these different types of mounting.

01:18:51.030 --> 01:18:55.080
So VMH, the aggression locus

01:18:55.080 --> 01:18:58.350
is actually active during
dominance mounting,

01:18:58.350 --> 01:19:02.310
and you can stimulate
mounting, dominance mounting,

01:19:02.310 --> 01:19:05.400
if you weakly activate VMH,

01:19:05.400 --> 01:19:08.820
whereas MPOA is most strongly activated

01:19:08.820 --> 01:19:10.770
during sexual mounting,

01:19:10.770 --> 01:19:12.780
and that's always accompanied

01:19:12.780 --> 01:19:14.670
by the ultrasonic vocalization.

01:19:14.670 --> 01:19:18.480
So this shows how difficult
and dangerous it can be

01:19:18.480 --> 01:19:23.370
to try to infer an animal's
state, or intent, or emotion,

01:19:23.370 --> 01:19:25.860
from the behavior that it's exhibiting

01:19:25.860 --> 01:19:29.100
because the same behavior can
mean very different things

01:19:29.100 --> 01:19:31.810
depending on the context of the
interaction with the animal.

01:19:31.810 --> 01:19:33.840
- And I would say, even more so

01:19:33.840 --> 01:19:37.620
with when that animal is a
human or is multiple humans.

01:19:37.620 --> 01:19:40.410
- That's right, and
there are many examples,

01:19:40.410 --> 01:19:44.400
animals show chasing to obtain food,

01:19:44.400 --> 01:19:47.070
a prey animal that they're
going to kill and eat,

01:19:47.070 --> 01:19:49.830
and they show chasing to obtain a mate

01:19:49.830 --> 01:19:51.570
that they're going to have sex with.

01:19:51.570 --> 01:19:55.530
And so the intent of the
chasing is completely different,

01:19:55.530 --> 01:19:57.360
and we don't know in all these cases

01:19:57.360 --> 01:19:59.490
whether there are separate circuits

01:19:59.490 --> 01:20:01.743
or common circuits that
are being activated.

01:20:02.670 --> 01:20:04.770
- I'm obsessed with dogs and dog breeds

01:20:04.770 --> 01:20:06.090
and et cetera, et cetera,

01:20:06.090 --> 01:20:08.700
and one thing I can tell you

01:20:08.700 --> 01:20:12.720
is that female dogs will mount and thrust.

01:20:12.720 --> 01:20:17.010
We had a female pit bull
mix, a very sweet dog,

01:20:17.010 --> 01:20:18.903
but in observing her,

01:20:19.807 --> 01:20:22.830
it convinced me that one can never assume

01:20:22.830 --> 01:20:26.400
that male dogs are more
aggressive than female dogs.

01:20:26.400 --> 01:20:27.447
It turns out, in talking to people

01:20:27.447 --> 01:20:31.260
who are quite skilled at dog
genetics and dog breeding,

01:20:31.260 --> 01:20:33.870
that there's a dominance
hierarchy within a litter

01:20:33.870 --> 01:20:38.467
and it crosses over
male-female delineations.

01:20:39.420 --> 01:20:41.790
So you can get a female in the
litter that's very dominant

01:20:41.790 --> 01:20:43.170
and a male that's very subordinate,

01:20:43.170 --> 01:20:45.840
and no one really knows what relates to.

01:20:45.840 --> 01:20:46.980
This is also why little dogs

01:20:46.980 --> 01:20:48.870
sometimes will get right up in the face

01:20:48.870 --> 01:20:50.580
of a big Doberman Pinscher.
- Mm.

01:20:50.580 --> 01:20:51.413
- And just start barking,

01:20:51.413 --> 01:20:53.040
which is an idiotic thing for it to do,

01:20:53.040 --> 01:20:56.160
but they can be dominant
over a much larger dog.

01:20:56.160 --> 01:20:57.660
- Hmm.
- Very strange,

01:20:57.660 --> 01:20:58.533
to me anyway.

01:20:59.460 --> 01:21:02.103
Female-female mounting,
do you observe it in mice?

01:21:03.090 --> 01:21:04.620
Are there known circuits,

01:21:04.620 --> 01:21:07.230
and what evokes female-female mounting,

01:21:07.230 --> 01:21:10.410
or female-to-male mounting if it occurs?

01:21:10.410 --> 01:21:15.410
- Good, yes, there are
clear examples of females

01:21:16.410 --> 01:21:19.020
displaying male-type mounting behavior

01:21:19.020 --> 01:21:20.520
towards other females.

01:21:20.520 --> 01:21:23.910
We see this most commonly in the lab

01:21:23.910 --> 01:21:27.390
where we are housing
females with their sisters,

01:21:27.390 --> 01:21:29.550
say three or four in a cage,

01:21:29.550 --> 01:21:33.450
we take one out and we
have her mate with a male,

01:21:33.450 --> 01:21:35.220
where the male's doing the mounting,

01:21:35.220 --> 01:21:37.020
now we take that female

01:21:37.020 --> 01:21:39.930
and we put her back in the
cage with her litter mates

01:21:39.930 --> 01:21:42.423
and she starts mounting them.

01:21:42.423 --> 01:21:45.930
Now, what the function of that is,

01:21:45.930 --> 01:21:47.700
if it has any function,

01:21:47.700 --> 01:21:51.120
or what it means, what's
driving it, we don't know,

01:21:51.120 --> 01:21:53.950
but we do know that if we stimulate

01:21:55.601 --> 01:21:58.230
the neurons that control mounting in males

01:21:58.230 --> 01:21:59.760
in the medial preoptic area,

01:21:59.760 --> 01:22:03.720
if we stimulate that same
population in females,

01:22:03.720 --> 01:22:07.140
it evokes male-type mounting

01:22:07.140 --> 01:22:09.660
towards either a male or a female target.

01:22:09.660 --> 01:22:11.400
In fact, we have a movie

01:22:11.400 --> 01:22:13.920
where we have a female

01:22:13.920 --> 01:22:16.530
that has just been mounted by a male,

01:22:16.530 --> 01:22:19.740
so the male's on top and she's underneath,

01:22:19.740 --> 01:22:24.120
and we stimulate that region
of MPOA in the female.

01:22:24.120 --> 01:22:27.390
And she crawls out from
underneath the male

01:22:27.390 --> 01:22:29.070
who has just mounted her,

01:22:29.070 --> 01:22:31.500
circles around behind him

01:22:31.500 --> 01:22:33.390
and climbs up on top of him

01:22:33.390 --> 01:22:36.510
and starts to try to mount
him and thrust at him.

01:22:36.510 --> 01:22:39.127
- That has a name online,
it's called a switch.

01:22:39.127 --> 01:22:40.650
[Andrew laughing]
- Is that right? [laughing]

01:22:40.650 --> 01:22:41.760
- [Andrew] Don't ask me how I know that.

01:22:41.760 --> 01:22:42.840
- Okay.

01:22:42.840 --> 01:22:47.043
- But it's a pretty, yeah,
it's a term that you hear.

01:22:48.090 --> 01:22:50.670
You also hear the term
topping from the bottom,

01:22:50.670 --> 01:22:52.440
which it sounds like
that is a literal topping

01:22:52.440 --> 01:22:53.273
from the bottom.
- I see.

01:22:53.273 --> 01:22:54.930
- That's a more of a psychological phrase,

01:22:54.930 --> 01:22:55.770
from what I hear.

01:22:55.770 --> 01:22:59.910
I have friends that are
educating me in this language,

01:22:59.910 --> 01:23:01.290
mostly because I find

01:23:01.290 --> 01:23:04.727
this kind of neurobiological
discussion fascinating.

01:23:04.727 --> 01:23:07.110
And at some point, right?

01:23:07.110 --> 01:23:09.930
I attempt, in my mind, to
superimpose observations

01:23:09.930 --> 01:23:11.320
from the online communities.

01:23:11.320 --> 01:23:12.900
- Yeah.
- That I'm told about

01:23:12.900 --> 01:23:14.460
and asked about to this,

01:23:14.460 --> 01:23:17.010
but I should point out,
it's always dangerous,

01:23:17.010 --> 01:23:18.240
and in fact, inappropriate

01:23:18.240 --> 01:23:20.730
to make a one-to-one link.
- Yes.

01:23:20.730 --> 01:23:23.610
- Humans, they maintain all
the same neural circuitry

01:23:23.610 --> 01:23:26.010
and pathways that we're
talking about today in mice,

01:23:26.010 --> 01:23:30.210
but that forebrain does
allow for context, et cetera.

01:23:30.210 --> 01:23:31.043
- Yep.
- Yeah.

01:23:31.043 --> 01:23:36.043
- So what the function
is of female mounting,

01:23:36.420 --> 01:23:39.540
I don't know, it could be a
type of dominance display.

01:23:39.540 --> 01:23:41.040
It's hard to measure that

01:23:41.040 --> 01:23:42.390
because people haven't worked

01:23:42.390 --> 01:23:45.060
on female-dominance hierarchies

01:23:45.060 --> 01:23:46.710
to the same extent that they've worked

01:23:46.710 --> 01:23:49.050
on male-dominance hierarchies,

01:23:49.050 --> 01:23:54.050
but it indicates that the
circuits for male-type mounting

01:23:54.060 --> 01:23:55.800
are there in females,

01:23:55.800 --> 01:24:00.240
as early work from Catherine
Dulac suggested some years ago.

01:24:00.240 --> 01:24:01.680
- Fascinating, fascinating.

01:24:01.680 --> 01:24:04.983
I love that paper because,
as you pointed out for chase,

01:24:06.930 --> 01:24:08.610
for mounting behavior, we see it

01:24:08.610 --> 01:24:11.400
and we think one thing specifically,

01:24:11.400 --> 01:24:12.780
and after hearing this result,

01:24:12.780 --> 01:24:14.910
actually, I'm not a big
fan of fight sports.

01:24:14.910 --> 01:24:16.830
I watch them occasionally
'cause friends are into them,

01:24:16.830 --> 01:24:21.300
but I've seen boxing matches, MMA matches,

01:24:21.300 --> 01:24:23.100
where at the end of a round,

01:24:23.100 --> 01:24:25.080
if someone felt that they dominated,

01:24:25.080 --> 01:24:27.780
they will do the unsportsmanlike thing

01:24:27.780 --> 01:24:30.240
of thrusting on the
back of the other person

01:24:30.240 --> 01:24:31.073
before they get off.
- Really?

01:24:31.073 --> 01:24:32.970
- Almost like, "I dominated you, and I'm,"

01:24:32.970 --> 01:24:35.040
so mimicking sexual-like behavior,

01:24:35.040 --> 01:24:37.320
but there's no reason to
think that it's sexual,

01:24:37.320 --> 01:24:38.460
but they're sending a message.
- Yeah.

01:24:38.460 --> 01:24:40.293
- Of dominance is what it implies.

01:24:41.580 --> 01:24:44.370
I'd love to talk about something

01:24:44.370 --> 01:24:46.200
slightly off from this circuitry,

01:24:46.200 --> 01:24:48.210
but I think that's
related to the circuitry,

01:24:48.210 --> 01:24:49.260
at least in some way,

01:24:49.260 --> 01:24:52.500
which is this structure that
I've always been fascinated by

01:24:52.500 --> 01:24:55.082
and I can't figure out
what the hell it's for,

01:24:55.082 --> 01:24:56.070
'cause it seems to be
involved in everything,

01:24:56.070 --> 01:25:00.270
which is the PAG, the periaqueductal gray,

01:25:00.270 --> 01:25:01.770
which is a little bit
further back in the brain,

01:25:01.770 --> 01:25:03.330
for people that don't know.

01:25:03.330 --> 01:25:05.010
It's been studied in the context of pain,

01:25:05.010 --> 01:25:06.570
it's been studied in the context

01:25:06.570 --> 01:25:08.490
of the so-called lordosis response,

01:25:08.490 --> 01:25:10.920
the receptivity or arching
of the back of the female

01:25:10.920 --> 01:25:13.533
to receive intromission
and mating from the male.

01:25:14.520 --> 01:25:16.443
How should we think about PAG?

01:25:17.370 --> 01:25:19.560
Clearly, it can't be
involved in everything,

01:25:19.560 --> 01:25:21.600
I'm guessing it's at least as complex

01:25:21.600 --> 01:25:22.590
as some of these other regions

01:25:22.590 --> 01:25:23.550
that we've been talking about,

01:25:23.550 --> 01:25:25.380
different types of neurons
controlling different things,

01:25:25.380 --> 01:25:27.810
but how does PAG play into this?

01:25:27.810 --> 01:25:29.940
In particular, I want to know,

01:25:29.940 --> 01:25:33.480
is there some mechanism of
pain modulation and control

01:25:33.480 --> 01:25:37.350
during fighting and/or mating?

01:25:37.350 --> 01:25:39.570
And the reason I ask is that,

01:25:39.570 --> 01:25:42.210
while I'm not a combat sports person,

01:25:42.210 --> 01:25:44.520
years ago, I did a little
bit of martial arts,

01:25:44.520 --> 01:25:46.770
and it always was impressive to me

01:25:46.770 --> 01:25:49.290
how little it hurt to get
punched during a fight

01:25:49.290 --> 01:25:51.900
and how much it hurt
afterwards, [laughing] right?

01:25:51.900 --> 01:25:54.900
So there clearly is some
endogenous pain control.

01:25:54.900 --> 01:25:56.370
- Yep.
- That then wears off,

01:25:56.370 --> 01:25:57.900
and then you feel beat up.

01:25:57.900 --> 01:25:58.733
- [David] Yep.

01:25:58.733 --> 01:26:00.720
- Or at least, in my case, I felt beat up.

01:26:00.720 --> 01:26:04.170
What's PAG doing vis-a-vis pain,

01:26:04.170 --> 01:26:06.270
and what's pain doing vis-a-vis
these other behaviors?

01:26:06.270 --> 01:26:07.440
- Good, good.

01:26:07.440 --> 01:26:09.250
So I think of PAG

01:26:10.110 --> 01:26:13.950
like a old-fashioned
telephone switchboard,

01:26:13.950 --> 01:26:17.430
where there are calls coming in,

01:26:17.430 --> 01:26:20.850
and then the cables have to
be punched into the right hole

01:26:20.850 --> 01:26:22.380
to get the information,

01:26:22.380 --> 01:26:26.490
to be routed to the right
recipient on the other end of it,

01:26:26.490 --> 01:26:30.600
because pretty much every
type of innate behavior

01:26:30.600 --> 01:26:34.047
you can think of has
had the PAG implicated.

01:26:34.047 --> 01:26:36.390
And there's a whole literature

01:26:36.390 --> 01:26:39.360
showing the involvement
of the PAG in fear,

01:26:39.360 --> 01:26:41.640
different regions of the PAG,

01:26:41.640 --> 01:26:43.380
the dorsal PAG is involved

01:26:43.380 --> 01:26:46.350
in panic-like behavior, running away,

01:26:46.350 --> 01:26:49.833
the ventral PAG is involved
in freezing behavior.

01:26:50.910 --> 01:26:55.910
Both the MPOA and VMH send
projections to the PAG,

01:26:56.460 --> 01:26:58.860
to different regions of the PAG.

01:26:58.860 --> 01:27:02.790
So in cross-section, I hate to say this,

01:27:02.790 --> 01:27:05.580
but in cross-section, the
PAG kind of looks like

01:27:05.580 --> 01:27:06.930
the water in a toilet

01:27:06.930 --> 01:27:09.750
when you're standing
over an open toilet bowl.

01:27:09.750 --> 01:27:11.460
- Mm-hmm.
- And if you imagine

01:27:11.460 --> 01:27:15.000
a clock face projected onto that,

01:27:15.000 --> 01:27:18.660
it's like the PAG has sectors,

01:27:18.660 --> 01:27:21.240
from one to 12, maybe even more of them,

01:27:21.240 --> 01:27:22.890
and in each of those sectors,

01:27:22.890 --> 01:27:24.330
you find different neurons

01:27:24.330 --> 01:27:26.850
from the hypothalamus are projecting.

01:27:26.850 --> 01:27:31.170
So could turn out that there
is a topographic arrangement

01:27:31.170 --> 01:27:34.170
along the dorsal-ventral axis of the PAG

01:27:34.170 --> 01:27:36.990
and the medial-lateral axis of the PAG

01:27:36.990 --> 01:27:39.840
that determines the type of behavior

01:27:39.840 --> 01:27:42.240
that will be emitted when neurons

01:27:42.240 --> 01:27:43.950
in that region are stimulated.

01:27:43.950 --> 01:27:46.020
And I think sort of all of the evidence

01:27:46.020 --> 01:27:47.790
is pointing in that direction,

01:27:47.790 --> 01:27:50.520
but by no means, has it been mapped out.

01:27:50.520 --> 01:27:54.000
Now, the thing that you
mentioned about it not hurting

01:27:54.000 --> 01:27:56.880
when you got beat up during martial arts,

01:27:56.880 --> 01:27:59.220
there is a well-known phenomenon

01:27:59.220 --> 01:28:03.780
called fear-induced analgesia,

01:28:03.780 --> 01:28:08.700
where when an animal is
in a high state of fear,

01:28:08.700 --> 01:28:11.190
like if it's trying to defend itself,

01:28:11.190 --> 01:28:16.170
there is a suppression of pain responses,

01:28:16.170 --> 01:28:20.640
and I'm not sure completely
about the mechanisms

01:28:20.640 --> 01:28:22.680
and how well that's understood,

01:28:22.680 --> 01:28:27.680
but for example, the adrenal
gland has a peptide in it

01:28:28.740 --> 01:28:32.160
that is released from the adrenal medulla,

01:28:32.160 --> 01:28:35.010
which controls the
fight-or-flight responses,

01:28:35.010 --> 01:28:38.910
and that peptide has analgesic activities.

01:28:38.910 --> 01:28:39.743
Now, whether.
- May I ask what that

01:28:39.743 --> 01:28:40.576
peptide is?
- It's called

01:28:40.576 --> 01:28:45.450
bovine adrenal medullary peptide
of 22 amino acid residues.

01:28:45.450 --> 01:28:47.160
And I only know about it

01:28:47.160 --> 01:28:49.470
because it activates a receptor

01:28:49.470 --> 01:28:51.540
that we discovered many years ago

01:28:51.540 --> 01:28:53.220
that's involved in pain,

01:28:53.220 --> 01:28:54.780
and we thought it promoted pain,

01:28:54.780 --> 01:28:57.660
but it turns out that this
actually inhibits pain,

01:28:57.660 --> 01:29:00.420
it's like an endogenous analgesic.

01:29:00.420 --> 01:29:05.370
Whether this is happening,
this type of analgesia

01:29:05.370 --> 01:29:07.380
is happening when an animal

01:29:07.380 --> 01:29:10.170
is engaged in offensive aggression

01:29:10.170 --> 01:29:14.400
or in mating behavior, I don't know,

01:29:14.400 --> 01:29:16.380
but it certainly is possible.

01:29:16.380 --> 01:29:20.460
And I don't know whether
these analgesic mechanisms

01:29:20.460 --> 01:29:22.440
are happening in the PAG,

01:29:22.440 --> 01:29:25.500
they could also be happening
a little further down

01:29:25.500 --> 01:29:26.550
in the spinal cord.

01:29:26.550 --> 01:29:29.970
The PAG is really continuous
with the spinal cord,

01:29:29.970 --> 01:29:33.870
if you just follow it down
towards the tail of an animal,

01:29:33.870 --> 01:29:36.453
you will wind up in the spinal cord.

01:29:38.291 --> 01:29:41.190
And so it could be that
there are influences acting

01:29:41.190 --> 01:29:43.980
at many levels on pain in the PAG

01:29:43.980 --> 01:29:46.320
and in the spinal cord as well.

01:29:46.320 --> 01:29:49.050
And it may well be known,
I just don't know it,

01:29:49.050 --> 01:29:51.750
I want to distinguish
clearly between things

01:29:51.750 --> 01:29:55.050
that are not known,
that I know are unknown,

01:29:55.050 --> 01:29:58.740
which is in a fairly small
area where I have expertise,

01:29:58.740 --> 01:30:00.270
from things that may be known,

01:30:00.270 --> 01:30:01.770
but I'm ignorant of them,

01:30:01.770 --> 01:30:03.510
because I just don't have a broad enough

01:30:03.510 --> 01:30:04.510
knowledge base to know that.

01:30:04.510 --> 01:30:08.193
- Sure, we appreciate those delineations.

01:30:09.240 --> 01:30:11.670
Thank you, PAG, I think
this description of it

01:30:11.670 --> 01:30:14.040
as an old-fashioned telephone switchboard,

01:30:14.040 --> 01:30:16.920
and now every time I look into
the toilet, I'll think about

01:30:16.920 --> 01:30:18.150
the periaqueductal gray.
- [laughing] That's right.

01:30:18.150 --> 01:30:19.200
- [Andrew] And every time I see an image

01:30:19.200 --> 01:30:20.220
of periaqueductal gray,

01:30:20.220 --> 01:30:21.053
I'll think about a toilet.
- That's right. [laughing]

01:30:21.053 --> 01:30:23.640
- That is an excellent description,

01:30:23.640 --> 01:30:25.170
because, in fact, I drew a circle

01:30:25.170 --> 01:30:26.250
with a little thing at the bottom.

01:30:26.250 --> 01:30:30.240
And well, I'll put a post
or link to a picture of PAG

01:30:30.240 --> 01:30:33.210
and you'll understand why
David and I are chuckling here,

01:30:33.210 --> 01:30:35.370
because, indeed, it looks like a toilet,

01:30:35.370 --> 01:30:36.783
when staring into a toilet.

01:30:38.280 --> 01:30:40.320
Tell us about tachykinin,

01:30:40.320 --> 01:30:41.640
I've talked about this a couple times

01:30:41.640 --> 01:30:42.900
on different podcast episodes

01:30:42.900 --> 01:30:47.370
because of its relationship
to social isolation,

01:30:47.370 --> 01:30:51.090
and in part, because
the podcast was launched

01:30:51.090 --> 01:30:53.640
during a time when there
was more social isolation.

01:30:54.630 --> 01:30:56.790
My understanding is that tachykinin,

01:30:56.790 --> 01:30:59.310
and you'll tell us what it is in a moment,

01:30:59.310 --> 01:31:01.800
is present in flies
and mice and in humans,

01:31:01.800 --> 01:31:05.820
and may do similar
things in those species.

01:31:05.820 --> 01:31:10.820
- That's right, so
tachykinin refers to a family

01:31:11.010 --> 01:31:12.870
of related neuropeptides.

01:31:12.870 --> 01:31:15.153
So these are brain chemicals,

01:31:16.110 --> 01:31:19.350
they're different from
dopamine and serotonin

01:31:19.350 --> 01:31:23.040
in that they're not
small, organic molecules,

01:31:23.040 --> 01:31:25.830
they're actually short pieces of protein

01:31:25.830 --> 01:31:28.740
that are directly encoded by genes

01:31:28.740 --> 01:31:31.140
that are active in specific neurons

01:31:31.140 --> 01:31:32.280
and not in others.

01:31:32.280 --> 01:31:34.050
And when those neurons are active,

01:31:34.050 --> 01:31:36.270
those neuropeptides are released

01:31:36.270 --> 01:31:38.850
together with classical transmitters,

01:31:38.850 --> 01:31:40.530
like glutamate, whatever.

01:31:40.530 --> 01:31:45.480
Tachykinins have been
famously implicated in pain,

01:31:45.480 --> 01:31:47.910
particularly Tachykinin-I,

01:31:47.910 --> 01:31:50.340
which is called Substance P,

01:31:50.340 --> 01:31:53.310
one of the original pain modulating,

01:31:53.310 --> 01:31:57.450
this is something that
promotes inflammatory pain.

01:31:57.450 --> 01:31:59.970
But there are other tachykinin genes,

01:31:59.970 --> 01:32:01.620
in mice, there are two,

01:32:01.620 --> 01:32:03.930
in humans, I think there are three,

01:32:03.930 --> 01:32:06.360
and in Drosophila, there's one.

01:32:06.360 --> 01:32:09.210
And the way we got into tachykinins

01:32:09.210 --> 01:32:12.510
is from studying aggression in flies.

01:32:12.510 --> 01:32:15.690
We thought, since neuropeptides

01:32:15.690 --> 01:32:20.220
have this remarkable parallel
evolutionary conservation

01:32:20.220 --> 01:32:22.020
of structure and function,

01:32:22.020 --> 01:32:25.410
like Neuropeptide Y controls feeding

01:32:25.410 --> 01:32:28.950
in worms, in flies and mice and in people.

01:32:28.950 --> 01:32:32.160
Oxytocin-like peptides
control reproduction

01:32:32.160 --> 01:32:35.550
in worms and mice and in people.

01:32:35.550 --> 01:32:39.240
We thought we might find
peptides that control aggression

01:32:39.240 --> 01:32:40.710
in flies and in people,

01:32:40.710 --> 01:32:44.670
and so we did a screen,
unbiased screen of peptides,

01:32:44.670 --> 01:32:48.737
and found, indeed, that
one of the tachykinins,

01:32:48.737 --> 01:32:52.980
Drosophila tachykinin, those
neurons when you activate them

01:32:52.980 --> 01:32:54.750
strongly promote aggression,

01:32:54.750 --> 01:32:57.510
and it depends on the
release of tachykinin.

01:32:57.510 --> 01:32:59.370
Now, the interesting thing is that,

01:32:59.370 --> 01:33:01.980
in flies, just like in people

01:33:01.980 --> 01:33:05.430
and practically any other social animal

01:33:05.430 --> 01:33:06.900
that shows aggression,

01:33:06.900 --> 01:33:10.380
social isolation increases aggressiveness.

01:33:10.380 --> 01:33:14.880
So putting a violent prisoner
in solitary confinement

01:33:14.880 --> 01:33:17.730
is absolutely the worst,
most counterproductive thing

01:33:17.730 --> 01:33:19.020
you could do to them.

01:33:19.020 --> 01:33:21.360
And indeed, we found in flies

01:33:21.360 --> 01:33:24.660
that social isolation increases the level

01:33:24.660 --> 01:33:26.850
of tachykinin in the brain,

01:33:26.850 --> 01:33:29.130
and if we shut that gene down,

01:33:29.130 --> 01:33:32.610
it prevents the isolation
from increasing aggression.

01:33:32.610 --> 01:33:35.640
So since my lab also works on mice,

01:33:35.640 --> 01:33:38.940
it was natural to see whether tachykinins

01:33:38.940 --> 01:33:42.240
might be upregulated in social isolation

01:33:42.240 --> 01:33:44.580
and whether they play
a role in aggression.

01:33:44.580 --> 01:33:45.630
And this is work done

01:33:45.630 --> 01:33:48.030
by a former postdoc, Moriel Zelikowsky,

01:33:48.030 --> 01:33:50.850
now at University of
Salt Lake City in Utah,

01:33:50.850 --> 01:33:52.950
and she found, remarkably,

01:33:52.950 --> 01:33:56.820
that when mice are socially
isolated for two weeks,

01:33:56.820 --> 01:34:00.180
there is this massive upregulation

01:34:00.180 --> 01:34:03.390
of Tachykinin-II in their brain.

01:34:03.390 --> 01:34:06.270
In fact, if you tag the peptide

01:34:06.270 --> 01:34:08.850
with a green fluorescent protein

01:34:08.850 --> 01:34:10.740
from a jellyfish, genetically,

01:34:10.740 --> 01:34:14.910
the brain looks green when
the mice are socially isolated

01:34:14.910 --> 01:34:17.700
'cause there's so much
of this stuff released.

01:34:17.700 --> 01:34:22.700
And she went on to show that
that increase in tachykinin

01:34:24.060 --> 01:34:27.780
is responsible for the
effect of social isolation

01:34:27.780 --> 01:34:29.790
to increase aggressiveness

01:34:29.790 --> 01:34:31.590
and to increase fear

01:34:31.590 --> 01:34:33.210
and to increase anxiety.

01:34:33.210 --> 01:34:35.220
And in fact, there are drugs

01:34:35.220 --> 01:34:38.130
that block the receptor for tachykinin

01:34:38.130 --> 01:34:41.130
which were tested in humans and abandoned

01:34:41.130 --> 01:34:43.050
because they had no efficacy

01:34:43.050 --> 01:34:45.540
in the tests that they were analyzed for.

01:34:45.540 --> 01:34:49.710
If you give those drugs to
a socially isolated mouse,

01:34:49.710 --> 01:34:53.370
it blocks all of the
effects of social isolation.

01:34:53.370 --> 01:34:55.290
It blocks the aggression,

01:34:55.290 --> 01:34:58.890
it blocks the increased fear
and the increased anxiety,

01:34:58.890 --> 01:35:02.490
and Moriel described it,
"The mice just look chill."

01:35:02.490 --> 01:35:05.160
It's not a sedative,
which is really important,

01:35:05.160 --> 01:35:08.190
it's not that the mice are going to sleep.

01:35:08.190 --> 01:35:13.170
Most remarkably is, once
you socially isolate a mouse

01:35:13.170 --> 01:35:14.580
and it becomes aggressive,

01:35:14.580 --> 01:35:17.760
you can never put it back in its cage

01:35:17.760 --> 01:35:19.620
with its brothers from its litter

01:35:19.620 --> 01:35:22.080
because it will kill them all overnight,

01:35:22.080 --> 01:35:24.360
but if you give it this drug,

01:35:24.360 --> 01:35:29.360
which is called osanetant,
that blocks Tachykinin-II,

01:35:29.640 --> 01:35:33.270
that mouse can be returned
to the cage with its brothers

01:35:33.270 --> 01:35:34.770
and will not attack them,

01:35:34.770 --> 01:35:38.670
and seems to be happy about
that for the rest of the time.

01:35:38.670 --> 01:35:42.510
So this is an incredibly
powerful effect of this drug,

01:35:42.510 --> 01:35:44.760
and I've been really interested

01:35:44.760 --> 01:35:49.260
in trying to get pharmaceutical
companies to test this drug,

01:35:49.260 --> 01:35:52.950
which has a really good
safety profile in humans,

01:35:52.950 --> 01:35:55.380
in testing it in people

01:35:55.380 --> 01:35:58.440
who are subjected to
social isolation stress

01:35:58.440 --> 01:36:00.570
or bereavement stress.

01:36:00.570 --> 01:36:02.940
And this is one of the areas

01:36:02.940 --> 01:36:06.900
where I learned an eye-opening lesson,

01:36:06.900 --> 01:36:09.660
as a basic scientist who naively thought

01:36:09.660 --> 01:36:11.430
that if you make a discovery

01:36:11.430 --> 01:36:15.180
and it has translational
applications to humans,

01:36:15.180 --> 01:36:16.860
that pharmaceutical companies

01:36:16.860 --> 01:36:19.980
are going to be falling all
over themselves to try it.

01:36:19.980 --> 01:36:22.110
And they are not interested,

01:36:22.110 --> 01:36:25.560
because once burned, twice shy,

01:36:25.560 --> 01:36:30.210
these drugs were tested for
efficacy in schizophrenia.

01:36:30.210 --> 01:36:32.220
I have no idea why,

01:36:32.220 --> 01:36:35.310
there's very little preclinical
data to suggest that.

01:36:35.310 --> 01:36:37.773
Not surprisingly, they failed.

01:36:39.517 --> 01:36:42.480
When a drug fails in
clinical trials in Phase 3,

01:36:42.480 --> 01:36:46.510
it costs $100 million to the company

01:36:47.610 --> 01:36:49.320
that carried out that clinical trial.

01:36:49.320 --> 01:36:54.240
So there's a huge slag heap
of discarded pharmaceuticals,

01:36:54.240 --> 01:36:58.290
many of them inhibitors
of neuropeptide action,

01:36:58.290 --> 01:37:02.220
that could be useful in other indications,

01:37:02.220 --> 01:37:04.080
such as the one we discovered,

01:37:04.080 --> 01:37:07.500
but there's a huge economic disincentive

01:37:07.500 --> 01:37:11.850
for pharmaceutical companies
to test them again,

01:37:11.850 --> 01:37:14.310
because the conclusion that they drew

01:37:14.310 --> 01:37:16.140
from all these failed tests,

01:37:16.140 --> 01:37:20.220
particularly in the 2010s and before that,

01:37:20.220 --> 01:37:22.740
is that the reason they failed

01:37:22.740 --> 01:37:26.550
is because animal experiments with drugs

01:37:26.550 --> 01:37:31.200
don't predict how humans
will respond to the drugs,

01:37:31.200 --> 01:37:35.100
and therefore, we shouldn't
try to extrapolate

01:37:35.100 --> 01:37:38.760
from any other data that we
get from animal experiments,

01:37:38.760 --> 01:37:40.830
mouse or rat experiments to humans,

01:37:40.830 --> 01:37:43.530
because they'll lead us
down the wrong track,

01:37:43.530 --> 01:37:45.960
and I think that that is probably wrong.

01:37:45.960 --> 01:37:48.240
In some cases, it may be right,

01:37:48.240 --> 01:37:51.180
but in other cases, there's
good reason to think,

01:37:51.180 --> 01:37:54.030
because these brain regions and molecules

01:37:54.030 --> 01:37:56.730
are so evolutionarily conserved

01:37:56.730 --> 01:38:00.510
that they ought to be playing
a similar role in humans.

01:38:00.510 --> 01:38:04.680
In fact, there is a paper showing that

01:38:04.680 --> 01:38:09.680
in humans that have borderline
personality disorder,

01:38:09.810 --> 01:38:11.490
there's a strong correlation

01:38:11.490 --> 01:38:15.510
between their self-reported
level of aggressiveness

01:38:15.510 --> 01:38:18.630
and serum levels of a tachykinin,

01:38:18.630 --> 01:38:20.670
in this case, Tachykinin-I,

01:38:20.670 --> 01:38:22.860
as detected by radioimmunoassay.

01:38:22.860 --> 01:38:25.080
This is work of Emil Coccaro,

01:38:25.080 --> 01:38:28.620
who's a clinical psychiatrist
at the University of Chicago.

01:38:28.620 --> 01:38:33.210
So there is a smoking gun in
the case of humans as well.

01:38:33.210 --> 01:38:36.960
And I was actually trying to interest

01:38:36.960 --> 01:38:38.790
a pharmaceutical company

01:38:38.790 --> 01:38:41.700
that was testing these drugs,

01:38:41.700 --> 01:38:44.910
actually, for treatment of hot flashes

01:38:44.910 --> 01:38:46.860
in females, in humans,

01:38:46.860 --> 01:38:49.860
where there is actually good animal data

01:38:49.860 --> 01:38:51.930
to think that it might be useful,

01:38:51.930 --> 01:38:55.110
but I realized that this clinical trial

01:38:55.110 --> 01:38:58.680
was going on during the COVID pandemic.

01:38:58.680 --> 01:38:59.887
And I approached him and said,

01:38:59.887 --> 01:39:02.820
"Look, nature may have
actually done for you

01:39:02.820 --> 01:39:05.010
the experiment that I want you to do,

01:39:05.010 --> 01:39:06.060
'cause some of the people

01:39:06.060 --> 01:39:08.100
who are getting drug or placebo

01:39:08.100 --> 01:39:10.590
are going to have been socially isolated

01:39:10.590 --> 01:39:12.090
and some of them will have not.

01:39:12.090 --> 01:39:14.760
Why don't you get them to
fill out questionnaires

01:39:14.760 --> 01:39:16.020
and see whether the ones

01:39:16.020 --> 01:39:18.870
who were given the drug
and socially isolated

01:39:18.870 --> 01:39:21.510
felt less stressed and less anxious

01:39:21.510 --> 01:39:24.000
than the ones who were
not socially isolated?"

01:39:24.000 --> 01:39:26.130
And they would not touch it,

01:39:26.130 --> 01:39:29.280
because they're in the
middle of a clinical trial

01:39:29.280 --> 01:39:31.770
for a different indication for this drug,

01:39:31.770 --> 01:39:35.190
and they have to report any observation

01:39:35.190 --> 01:39:37.320
that they make about that drug

01:39:37.320 --> 01:39:38.970
in their patient population.

01:39:38.970 --> 01:39:41.430
So if they were to ask these questions

01:39:41.430 --> 01:39:43.927
and get an unfavorable answer,

01:39:43.927 --> 01:39:45.720
"Oh my God, I felt even worse

01:39:45.720 --> 01:39:47.970
when I took this drug and I was isolated,"

01:39:47.970 --> 01:39:51.180
they would be obliged to
report that to the FDA

01:39:51.180 --> 01:39:53.160
and that could torpedo the chances

01:39:53.160 --> 01:39:54.810
for the drug being approved

01:39:54.810 --> 01:39:56.880
in the thing that it was
in clinical trials for.

01:39:56.880 --> 01:40:00.970
So it's better not to ask and not to know

01:40:02.370 --> 01:40:05.160
than it is to try to
find out more information

01:40:05.160 --> 01:40:07.770
that could lead to another
clinical indication.

01:40:07.770 --> 01:40:12.660
So I remain convinced
that this family of drugs

01:40:12.660 --> 01:40:15.210
could have very powerful uses

01:40:15.210 --> 01:40:19.530
in treating some forms
of stress-induced anxiety

01:40:19.530 --> 01:40:21.240
or aggressiveness in humans,

01:40:21.240 --> 01:40:24.420
but it's just very difficult,
for economic reasons,

01:40:24.420 --> 01:40:26.970
to find a way to get
somebody to test that.

01:40:26.970 --> 01:40:30.480
- Yeah, a true shame that
these companies won't do this,

01:40:30.480 --> 01:40:34.170
and especially given the fact
that many of these drugs exist

01:40:34.170 --> 01:40:36.900
and their safety profiles are established,

01:40:36.900 --> 01:40:37.890
'cause that's always

01:40:37.890 --> 01:40:39.570
a serious consideration.
- Yep.

01:40:39.570 --> 01:40:41.793
- When embarking on a clinical trial.

01:40:42.630 --> 01:40:44.460
Perhaps in hearing this discussion,

01:40:44.460 --> 01:40:46.320
someone out there will understand

01:40:46.320 --> 01:40:49.620
the key importance of this
and will reach out to us,

01:40:49.620 --> 01:40:52.290
we'll provide ways to do that,

01:40:52.290 --> 01:40:55.170
to get such a study going in humans.

01:40:55.170 --> 01:40:57.510
Because I think if enough laboratories

01:40:57.510 --> 01:40:59.820
ran small-scale clinical trials,

01:40:59.820 --> 01:41:02.040
pharma certainly would
perk up their ears, right?

01:41:02.040 --> 01:41:04.050
I mean, they're so strategic.

01:41:04.050 --> 01:41:05.640
- Yep.
- Sometimes to their own.

01:41:05.640 --> 01:41:07.560
- I mean, I would like to say also,

01:41:07.560 --> 01:41:10.320
I'd like to see this tested on pets.

01:41:10.320 --> 01:41:13.050
I mean, there's a huge
number of pets right now

01:41:13.050 --> 01:41:15.630
that are suffering separation anxiety

01:41:15.630 --> 01:41:18.540
because humans bought
them to keep them company

01:41:18.540 --> 01:41:20.016
during the COVID pandemic,

01:41:20.016 --> 01:41:21.729
and now they're home alone.
- And now they're home alone,

01:41:21.729 --> 01:41:22.562
yeah.
- Okay?

01:41:22.562 --> 01:41:24.540
And if this thing works in mice,

01:41:24.540 --> 01:41:26.370
there's certainly a higher chance

01:41:26.370 --> 01:41:28.980
it's going to work in dogs or in cats

01:41:28.980 --> 01:41:30.750
than it is going to work in humans.

01:41:30.750 --> 01:41:33.720
And if it did, that would
be even more encouragement

01:41:33.720 --> 01:41:35.670
to continue along those lines.

01:41:35.670 --> 01:41:39.180
People sometimes forget that
although we work on animals

01:41:39.180 --> 01:41:41.490
and we ultimately want
to understand humans,

01:41:41.490 --> 01:41:43.680
we care about how our results

01:41:43.680 --> 01:41:46.740
apply to the welfare of animals as well,

01:41:46.740 --> 01:41:48.750
and particularly domestic pets,

01:41:48.750 --> 01:41:51.960
which is a multi-billion-dollar industry

01:41:51.960 --> 01:41:52.890
in this country.

01:41:52.890 --> 01:41:56.310
So if there is ways that they
can be made to feel better

01:41:56.310 --> 01:41:59.520
when they're separated from their owners,

01:41:59.520 --> 01:42:01.290
that would certainly be a good thing.

01:42:01.290 --> 01:42:03.810
- Absolutely, we will put out the call,

01:42:03.810 --> 01:42:05.040
we are putting out the call,

01:42:05.040 --> 01:42:08.313
and I know for sure
there will be a response.

01:42:10.500 --> 01:42:13.410
Just underscoring what we've
been talking about even more,

01:42:13.410 --> 01:42:16.710
every time we hear
about a school shooting,

01:42:16.710 --> 01:42:18.030
like in Texas recently,

01:42:18.030 --> 01:42:19.650
or I happened to be in New York

01:42:19.650 --> 01:42:22.100
during the time when there
was a subway shooting.

01:42:23.010 --> 01:42:28.010
For whatever reason, I listened
to the book about Columbine,

01:42:28.140 --> 01:42:30.000
that went into a very detailed way

01:42:30.000 --> 01:42:33.270
about the origin of those
boys that committed that,

01:42:33.270 --> 01:42:35.253
and every single time,

01:42:37.080 --> 01:42:39.180
the person who commits those acts

01:42:39.180 --> 01:42:40.380
is socially isolated,

01:42:40.380 --> 01:42:41.460
as far as I know.
- Yeah, yeah.

01:42:41.460 --> 01:42:42.990
- There might be some exceptions there.

01:42:42.990 --> 01:42:44.310
And sometimes this crosses over

01:42:44.310 --> 01:42:45.450
with other mental health issues,

01:42:45.450 --> 01:42:48.317
but sometimes no, no apparent
mental health issues.

01:42:48.317 --> 01:42:51.240
So social isolation clearly drives

01:42:51.240 --> 01:42:54.330
powerful neurochemical and
neuro biological changes,

01:42:54.330 --> 01:42:57.030
I really hope that Tachykinin-I and II,

01:42:57.030 --> 01:42:57.960
those are the main ones

01:42:57.960 --> 01:42:58.830
in humans?
- Yeah, yeah.

01:42:58.830 --> 01:43:01.410
- Will be explored in more detail.

01:43:01.410 --> 01:43:03.900
Also, I didn't know that
Tachykinin-I is Substance P.

01:43:03.900 --> 01:43:04.818
- Yes.
- And Substance P

01:43:04.818 --> 01:43:05.651
is Tachykinin-I.
- Yes.

01:43:05.651 --> 01:43:08.070
Tachykinin-I is the gene name,

01:43:08.070 --> 01:43:12.240
and Tachykinin-II, in humans,
is called Neurokinin B,

01:43:12.240 --> 01:43:14.160
that's the name of the protein.

01:43:14.160 --> 01:43:16.110
I just refer to it by the gene name

01:43:16.110 --> 01:43:17.280
'cause it makes it easier

01:43:17.280 --> 01:43:18.780
and I don't have to keep remembering

01:43:18.780 --> 01:43:20.310
two names for each thing.

01:43:20.310 --> 01:43:22.233
- And if I'm not mistaken,

01:43:23.160 --> 01:43:25.560
you put yourself in the
company of geneticists

01:43:25.560 --> 01:43:28.170
because your original
training was in genetics,

01:43:28.170 --> 01:43:30.000
immunology and areas

01:43:30.000 --> 01:43:31.530
related to that.
- It was in cell biology,

01:43:31.530 --> 01:43:34.680
and I didn't actually have
formal training in genetics

01:43:34.680 --> 01:43:35.880
as a graduate student,

01:43:35.880 --> 01:43:38.130
but I think I'm a geneticist at heart,

01:43:38.130 --> 01:43:41.250
that's just the way I like
to think about things.

01:43:41.250 --> 01:43:44.820
And when I started working
on flies, that sort of,

01:43:44.820 --> 01:43:46.920
I came out of the closet as a geneticist,

01:43:46.920 --> 01:43:48.180
as it were. [laughing]

01:43:48.180 --> 01:43:50.850
- Wonderful, as long as
we're talking about humans,

01:43:50.850 --> 01:43:51.750
I'd love to get your thoughts

01:43:51.750 --> 01:43:53.160
about human studies of emotion.

01:43:53.160 --> 01:43:55.140
I know you wrote this
book with Ralph Adolphs,

01:43:55.140 --> 01:43:57.330
you have this new book, which
we'll provide a link to,

01:43:57.330 --> 01:44:01.085
which I've read front to
back twice, it's phenomenal.

01:44:01.085 --> 01:44:01.918
- Thank you.
- I've mentioned it before

01:44:01.918 --> 01:44:03.270
on the podcast, it's really,

01:44:03.270 --> 01:44:05.100
there are books that are worth reading,

01:44:05.100 --> 01:44:06.600
and then there are books
that are important,

01:44:06.600 --> 01:44:08.190
and I think this book is truly important

01:44:08.190 --> 01:44:10.770
for the general population
to read and understand,

01:44:10.770 --> 01:44:13.200
and neuroscientists should read
and understand the contents,

01:44:13.200 --> 01:44:17.010
because we, as a culture,

01:44:17.010 --> 01:44:19.470
are way off in terms of
how we think about emotions

01:44:19.470 --> 01:44:21.300
and states and behaviors.

01:44:21.300 --> 01:44:22.440
So we'll put a link to that,

01:44:22.440 --> 01:44:25.830
it's really worth the time
and energy to read it,

01:44:25.830 --> 01:44:27.000
and it's written beautifully,

01:44:27.000 --> 01:44:27.833
I should say.
- Thank you.

01:44:27.833 --> 01:44:30.240
- Very accessible even for non-scientists.

01:44:30.240 --> 01:44:35.240
There's a heat map diagram in
that book that I think about,

01:44:35.550 --> 01:44:38.610
this is a heat map diagram
of subjective reports

01:44:38.610 --> 01:44:43.610
that people gave of where
they experience an emotion,

01:44:43.710 --> 01:44:45.660
or a feeling, a somatic feeling,

01:44:45.660 --> 01:44:48.480
in their body, or in their head, or both,

01:44:48.480 --> 01:44:52.350
when they are angry, sad, calm, lonely,

01:44:52.350 --> 01:44:53.790
et cetera, et cetera.

01:44:53.790 --> 01:44:56.460
And I wouldn't want people
to think that those heat maps

01:44:56.460 --> 01:45:00.870
were generated by any
physiological measurement,

01:45:00.870 --> 01:45:01.830
because they were not.

01:45:01.830 --> 01:45:04.890
And yet, I don't think
we can have a discussion

01:45:04.890 --> 01:45:06.840
about emotions and states

01:45:06.840 --> 01:45:09.150
and the sorts of behaviors
that we're talking about today

01:45:09.150 --> 01:45:11.010
without thinking about the body also.

01:45:11.010 --> 01:45:11.843
- [David] Yep.

01:45:11.843 --> 01:45:12.840
- And I'm not coming to this

01:45:12.840 --> 01:45:14.990
as a Northern California, mind-body.

01:45:14.990 --> 01:45:16.344
- Yeah.
- I've been to Esalen once.

01:45:16.344 --> 01:45:17.400
[David laughing]
I didn't go in the baths,

01:45:17.400 --> 01:45:18.960
I went there, I gave a talk and I left.

01:45:18.960 --> 01:45:20.490
It is very beautiful.

01:45:20.490 --> 01:45:21.600
If anyone wants to know
what it looks like,

01:45:21.600 --> 01:45:24.663
I think that final scene of
"Mad Men" is shot at Esalen,

01:45:25.650 --> 01:45:26.610
it's a very beautiful place.

01:45:26.610 --> 01:45:31.610
And yet, mind-body, to me, is
a neurobiological construct.

01:45:31.800 --> 01:45:32.700
- Yes.
- Because the nervous system

01:45:32.700 --> 01:45:35.100
extends through out of the cranial vault

01:45:35.100 --> 01:45:36.000
and into the spinal cord.
- Yeah.

01:45:36.000 --> 01:45:38.490
- And body and back and forth, okay.

01:45:38.490 --> 01:45:43.470
How should we think about
the body, in terms of states?

01:45:43.470 --> 01:45:45.780
And at some point, I'd
love for you to comment

01:45:45.780 --> 01:45:47.580
on that heat map experiment,

01:45:47.580 --> 01:45:50.280
because it does seem that
there's some regularity

01:45:50.280 --> 01:45:52.860
as to where people experience emotions.

01:45:52.860 --> 01:45:54.570
When people are in a rage, for instance,

01:45:54.570 --> 01:45:58.080
they seem to feel it both in
their gut and in their head,

01:45:58.080 --> 01:46:00.570
it seems, on average.

01:46:00.570 --> 01:46:05.280
And people love to
extrapolate to gut intuition

01:46:05.280 --> 01:46:08.730
or that the chakras or
anger is in the stomach,

01:46:08.730 --> 01:46:11.760
and this goes to Eastern
medicine, et cetera.

01:46:11.760 --> 01:46:13.920
How should we think about mind-body

01:46:13.920 --> 01:46:15.000
in the context of states,

01:46:15.000 --> 01:46:16.770
and think about it as scientists,

01:46:16.770 --> 01:46:20.040
maybe even as neuroscientists
or geneticists?

01:46:20.040 --> 01:46:23.670
- Good, so for the answer
to the first question

01:46:23.670 --> 01:46:25.110
about the heat maps

01:46:25.110 --> 01:46:28.440
and people associating
certain parts of their body

01:46:28.440 --> 01:46:30.840
with certain emotional feelings,

01:46:30.840 --> 01:46:33.150
this goes back to something

01:46:33.150 --> 01:46:35.640
called the somatic marker hypothesis,

01:46:35.640 --> 01:46:38.280
that was proposed by Antonio Damasio,

01:46:38.280 --> 01:46:41.730
who is a neurologist at USC,

01:46:41.730 --> 01:46:45.450
the idea that our subjective feeling

01:46:45.450 --> 01:46:48.090
of a particular emotion is,

01:46:48.090 --> 01:46:53.090
in part, associated with a sensation

01:46:53.340 --> 01:46:57.450
of something happening in a
particular part of our body,

01:46:57.450 --> 01:47:00.000
the gut, the heart,

01:47:00.000 --> 01:47:01.230
I don't see the liver

01:47:01.230 --> 01:47:06.230
invoked very much in
emotional characterization,

01:47:06.660 --> 01:47:07.493
but.
- But gall

01:47:07.493 --> 01:47:09.330
and the gallbladder.
- Yes.

01:47:09.330 --> 01:47:10.369
- Somebody having a lot of gall.

01:47:10.369 --> 01:47:11.202
- That's right.
- I don't know why I make

01:47:11.202 --> 01:47:12.035
a fist when I say that.

01:47:12.035 --> 01:47:12.868
- Right.
- But I'm guessing

01:47:12.868 --> 01:47:14.233
the gall bladder is shaped like a fist.

01:47:14.233 --> 01:47:15.600
[Andrew laughing]
- That's right,

01:47:15.600 --> 01:47:20.250
and if there is a physiology
underlying these heat maps,

01:47:20.250 --> 01:47:22.800
it could reflect increased blood flow

01:47:22.800 --> 01:47:24.300
to these different structures.

01:47:24.300 --> 01:47:28.050
And that, in turn, reflects
what you were talking about,

01:47:28.050 --> 01:47:30.840
that is, emotion definitely involves

01:47:30.840 --> 01:47:33.720
communication between
the brain and the body,

01:47:33.720 --> 01:47:36.840
and it's bidirectional communication,

01:47:36.840 --> 01:47:41.250
and it's mediated by the
peripheral nervous system,

01:47:41.250 --> 01:47:44.490
the sympathetic and the
parasympathetic nervous system,

01:47:44.490 --> 01:47:47.430
which control heart rate, for example,

01:47:47.430 --> 01:47:50.190
blood vessel, blood pressure.

01:47:50.190 --> 01:47:54.690
And those neurons receive
input from the hypothalamus

01:47:54.690 --> 01:47:56.880
and other brain regions,

01:47:56.880 --> 01:48:00.330
central brain regions that
control their activity.

01:48:00.330 --> 01:48:04.980
And when the brain is put
in a particular state,

01:48:04.980 --> 01:48:08.970
it activates sympathetic
and parasympathetic neurons,

01:48:08.970 --> 01:48:13.740
which have effects on the
heart and on blood pressure,

01:48:13.740 --> 01:48:18.120
and these, in turn,
feed back onto the brain

01:48:18.120 --> 01:48:20.010
through the sensory system.

01:48:20.010 --> 01:48:23.820
And a large part of this
bidirectional communication

01:48:23.820 --> 01:48:27.180
is also mediated through the vagus nerve,

01:48:27.180 --> 01:48:30.810
which many of your listeners and viewers

01:48:30.810 --> 01:48:31.680
may have heard about

01:48:31.680 --> 01:48:35.670
because it's become a topic
of intense activity now.

01:48:35.670 --> 01:48:38.160
People have known for a long time,

01:48:38.160 --> 01:48:43.160
so the vagus nerve is a
bundle of nerve fibers

01:48:43.530 --> 01:48:47.250
that comes out basically of your skull,

01:48:47.250 --> 01:48:49.440
out of the central nervous system,

01:48:49.440 --> 01:48:54.440
and then sends fibers
into your heart, your gut,

01:48:55.560 --> 01:48:58.203
all sorts of visceral organs.

01:48:59.130 --> 01:49:01.440
And that information is both,

01:49:01.440 --> 01:49:04.710
you used the words
earlier in our discussion,

01:49:04.710 --> 01:49:06.930
afferent and efferent.

01:49:06.930 --> 01:49:11.700
So the vagal fibers sense things

01:49:11.700 --> 01:49:13.830
that are happening in the body,

01:49:13.830 --> 01:49:16.890
so the reason you feel your stomach

01:49:16.890 --> 01:49:19.530
tied up in knots if you're tense

01:49:19.530 --> 01:49:21.600
is that those vagal fibers

01:49:21.600 --> 01:49:25.860
are sensing the contraction
of the gut muscles,

01:49:25.860 --> 01:49:27.720
and they're also afferents,

01:49:27.720 --> 01:49:31.110
which means that information
coming out of the brain

01:49:31.110 --> 01:49:34.740
can influence those
peripheral organs as well.

01:49:34.740 --> 01:49:37.080
And there's work from a number of labs,

01:49:37.080 --> 01:49:40.350
just in the last six months or so,

01:49:40.350 --> 01:49:44.820
where people are starting
to decode the components

01:49:44.820 --> 01:49:48.240
of the different fibers
in the vagus nerve.

01:49:48.240 --> 01:49:51.210
And it's amazing how much specificity is,

01:49:51.210 --> 01:49:55.320
there are specific vagal
nerves that go to the lung,

01:49:55.320 --> 01:49:57.570
that control breathing responses,

01:49:57.570 --> 01:50:01.590
that go to the gut,
that go to other organs.

01:50:01.590 --> 01:50:06.210
It's almost like a set
of color-coded lines,

01:50:06.210 --> 01:50:08.070
labeled lines for those things.

01:50:08.070 --> 01:50:11.970
And now how those vagal afferents

01:50:11.970 --> 01:50:16.560
play a role in the playing
out of emotion states

01:50:16.560 --> 01:50:18.270
is a fascinating question

01:50:18.270 --> 01:50:21.660
that people are just beginning
to scrape the surface of.

01:50:21.660 --> 01:50:23.760
But I think what's exciting now

01:50:23.760 --> 01:50:25.950
is that people are going
to be developing tools

01:50:25.950 --> 01:50:28.890
that will allow us to turn on or turn off

01:50:28.890 --> 01:50:33.300
specific subsets of fibers
within the vagus nerve

01:50:33.300 --> 01:50:37.020
and ask how that affects
particular emotional behaviors.

01:50:37.020 --> 01:50:38.640
So you're absolutely right,

01:50:38.640 --> 01:50:41.310
this brain-body connection is critical,

01:50:41.310 --> 01:50:43.980
not just for the gut, but for the heart,

01:50:43.980 --> 01:50:48.030
for the lungs, for all kinds
of other parts of your body,

01:50:48.030 --> 01:50:50.520
and Darwin recognized that as well.

01:50:50.520 --> 01:50:54.660
And I think it's a central
feature of emotion state,

01:50:54.660 --> 01:50:56.340
and I think, what underlies

01:50:56.340 --> 01:50:59.820
our subjective feelings of an emotion.

01:50:59.820 --> 01:51:02.520
- Incredible, well, David, I have to say,

01:51:02.520 --> 01:51:04.290
as a true fan of the work

01:51:04.290 --> 01:51:07.050
that your lab has been
doing over so many decades,

01:51:07.050 --> 01:51:09.120
and first of all, I was delighted

01:51:09.120 --> 01:51:10.712
when you stopped working on stem cells.

01:51:10.712 --> 01:51:11.550
[David laughing]
Not because you weren't doing

01:51:11.550 --> 01:51:12.540
incredible work there,

01:51:12.540 --> 01:51:14.550
but because I saw a talk

01:51:14.550 --> 01:51:17.170
where you showed a movie of an octopus

01:51:18.420 --> 01:51:19.860
spitting out, or not spitting,

01:51:19.860 --> 01:51:22.320
but squirting out a bunch
of ink and escaping,

01:51:22.320 --> 01:51:23.250
and you said you were going to work

01:51:23.250 --> 01:51:25.560
on things of the sort that
we're talking about today,

01:51:25.560 --> 01:51:29.070
fear, aggression, mating
behaviors, social behaviors.

01:51:29.070 --> 01:51:31.410
It's been incredible to see the
work that your lab has done,

01:51:31.410 --> 01:51:33.100
and I know I speak on behalf

01:51:35.632 --> 01:51:37.080
of a tremendous number of people

01:51:37.080 --> 01:51:39.240
when I say thank you for taking time

01:51:39.240 --> 01:51:40.740
out of your important schedule

01:51:40.740 --> 01:51:42.690
to share with us what you've learned.

01:51:42.690 --> 01:51:45.480
My last question is a simple one,

01:51:45.480 --> 01:51:47.160
which is, will you come back

01:51:47.160 --> 01:51:48.870
and talk to us again in the future

01:51:48.870 --> 01:51:50.820
about the additional
work that's sure to come?

01:51:50.820 --> 01:51:52.290
- I would be happy to do that,

01:51:52.290 --> 01:51:55.710
and I really have
appreciated your questions,

01:51:55.710 --> 01:51:57.450
they've all been right on the money,

01:51:57.450 --> 01:52:00.810
you've hit all of the
critical, important issues

01:52:00.810 --> 01:52:01.860
in this field.

01:52:01.860 --> 01:52:05.610
And you've uncovered what is known,

01:52:05.610 --> 01:52:06.810
the little bit is known,

01:52:06.810 --> 01:52:08.820
and how much is not known,

01:52:08.820 --> 01:52:13.110
and I think it's important to
emphasize the unknown things,

01:52:13.110 --> 01:52:16.740
because that's what the next
generation of neuroscientists

01:52:16.740 --> 01:52:18.090
has to solve.

01:52:18.090 --> 01:52:19.320
And so I hope this will help

01:52:19.320 --> 01:52:22.470
to attract young people into this field,

01:52:22.470 --> 01:52:24.270
because it's so important,

01:52:24.270 --> 01:52:27.600
particularly for our
understanding of mental illness

01:52:27.600 --> 01:52:31.650
and mental health and psychiatry,

01:52:31.650 --> 01:52:35.040
we've got to figure
out how emotion systems

01:52:35.040 --> 01:52:37.920
are controlled in a causal way

01:52:37.920 --> 01:52:39.870
if we ever want to improve

01:52:39.870 --> 01:52:42.540
on the psychiatric
treatments that we have now,

01:52:42.540 --> 01:52:43.890
and that's going to require

01:52:43.890 --> 01:52:46.710
the next generation of
people coming into the field.

01:52:46.710 --> 01:52:48.810
- Absolutely, I second that.

01:52:48.810 --> 01:52:50.340
Well, thank you, it's been a delight.

01:52:50.340 --> 01:52:53.460
- Thank you, great, really appreciate it.

01:52:53.460 --> 01:52:54.600
- Thank you for joining me today

01:52:54.600 --> 01:52:56.640
for my discussion with Dr. David Anderson.

01:52:56.640 --> 01:52:58.807
Please also be sure to
check out his new book,

01:52:58.807 --> 01:53:01.830
"The Nature of the Beast:
How Emotions Guide Us".

01:53:01.830 --> 01:53:03.840
It's a truly masterful exploration

01:53:03.840 --> 01:53:05.610
of the biology and psychology

01:53:05.610 --> 01:53:07.140
behind what we call emotions

01:53:07.140 --> 01:53:09.030
and states of mind and body.

